/* Wau-Bun — Part 3, the same scenes retold in a modern
   horror-suspense voice: the events, people, places and turns of the 1856
   text, in present-day English with the dread it actually carried. Original
   prose written for this app — nothing is quoted from any other book.
   Generated — do not hand-edit; the passages live in wau-bun/dark/<scene>.txt.
   Loaded only when a reader opens this mode. */
var WAUBUN_DARK_PART3 = {
  p3s1: ["Most of the command had already gone ahead of us. A day or two before we were to leave, they had started overland for Green Bay by way of Fort Winnebago, driving the cattle that belonged to the officers and the soldiers, and the noise of all that went out of the town and did not come back. Lieutenant Foster stayed behind. He wanted the pleasure of traveling with our party and was willing to wait for it. We waited too — long enough to see the rest of our family safely aboard the Napoleon and watch her stand out. After that there was nothing left to hold us. We turned our faces homeward again.", "We took a little bound-girl with us. Josette, ten years old, quick and bright and pretty, the daughter of Ouilmette — a Frenchman who had been living here at the time of the Massacre — and of a Pottowattamie mother. She had been schooled at the St. Joseph's mission under Mr. McCoy. The idea that she was going all the way to the Portage with Monsieur and Madame John made her shine.", "We had a negro boy as well. Harry. Mr. Kercheval had brought him up from Kentucky the year before, and in the crossing from a slave State to a free one, Harry's position quietly changed its shape: he could be nothing now but an indentured servant. He was about to enter Dr. Wolcott's household, and before that could happen he had to choose a guardian. They brought him into the parlor, where the family had gathered, and explained it to him. My husband was a young man then, home on a visit. \"Now, Harry,\" they said to him, \"you must choose your guardian.\" Every person in that room expected the boy to name whichever of them carried the most years and the most dignity. Harry rolled his great eyes around the circle of faces. He let his head hang over to one side. Then he said, \"I'll have Master John for my guardian.\"", "From that day forward Harry considered himself, in some measure, to belong to Master John — and when the family in Chicago broke up, he came over to our establishment as naturally as water finding its level.", "There were three ladies in the traveling party: our mother, our sister Mrs. Helm, and myself. I had spent some of the last days of the visit thinking about the sun and the prairie wind and what they do to a face left out in them, and I had made each of us a mask out of brown linen, cut to fit — openings for the eyes, the nose, the mouth, shaped to our own features. Then, because there was no reason to stop at merely strange, I had worked eyebrows and eyelashes into them in black silk, and a black ring around the hole for the mouth. They gathered in plaits under the chin and tied above and below, and they shut out the sun and the wind completely. Nothing more frightful can be imagined than the three of us standing there fully equipped. We argued about it. The argument was over which of us should be called the ugliest.", "We left in a wash of good wishes and laughter from the few acquaintances we had left. The wagon had a pair of excellent traveling horses; sister Margaret and I had the best pacers the country could produce; and we rode off toward the Aux Plaines in high spirits, with our old friend Billy Caldwell — the Sau-ga-nash — and our brother Robert and Gholson Kercheval riding out with us as far as that."],
  p3s2: ["There was almost nobody at Barney Lawton's when we got there — a Frenchman, and a small number of Indians. My sister and I got down and went into the house. The door stood open. Two Indians sat on the floor, smoking, and they lifted their eyes as we came into the light of the doorway. I will never forget what came over both their faces. Wonder first, and then horror. The lips of one of them went slack and his pipe dropped out of them onto the floor. Their eyes seemed to be starting out of their heads. They raised their hands, spread wide, as though to push us back out of the room, and said, slowly, \"Manitou!\" A spirit.", "We pulled up our masks and came forward smiling, with our hands out, and they were on their feet in an instant, letting out a cry of pure delight at the sight of two faces they knew.", "\"Bon-jour, bon-jour, Maman!\" And they went straight out the door to tell the others what had just happened to them.", "Our ride that afternoon lay over a prairie that ran away to the northeast with nothing living on the whole broad face of it. Nothing on the ground, at least. Over our heads flew and circled uncountable flocks of curlews, screaming their wild notes to the listening waste.", "Their cry is shrill and particular — crack, crack, crack, rackety, rackety, rackety — and coming out of dozens of throats at once, sometimes stooping down close enough to graze the ear, it became after a while almost more than we could stand. They seemed to have lost their senses over the excitement of so unusual and splendid a procession moving through their desolate country."],
  p3s3: ["The horses picked up their pace as we came toward a wooded knoll, and that was how we knew where we would be sleeping. They always know. Some instinct in them reads the ground and tells them where a camp belongs, and this one was as lovely a spot as anything I could have imagined.", "Down near the lake the cold, late season had kept the trees a pale unhealthy yellow, the color of goslings not yet fledged. Here they were breaking into full leaf. The ground under them was carpeted so thickly with flowers that we could hardly stand to think of what we were about to do to it — fell a tree, pitch a tent, put our weight down in the middle of all that. The birds were giving out their sweetest notes in the warm, lingering light. The opening buds of the young hickory and the sassafras had filled the whole hollow with perfume.", "After a day's ride in the glare of the sun, nothing could have been better than this. The children lost their heads over it. They wanted to be useful and kept inventing ways to be — stacking the saddles, breaking boughs for the fire, running the little kettles down for water and carrying them back to Petaille and Lecuyer, the Frenchmen, who were getting our supper together.", "What entertained them past all bearing was the way the horses moved once they had been spancelled — the short, ridiculous, hobbled step of them. Everything was new to our little nephew Edwin. Josette had already made more than one horseback trip to St. Joseph, and she took on all the dignity of a seasoned traveler, explaining to him whatever struck him as strange or unaccountable.", "They were not the last ones up when the call came the next morning — \"how! how!\"", "The fire was built up again, breakfast was started, and the Frenchmen went off to bring in the horses so we could get an early start.", "Harry and Josette did their parts, under our direction, and the meal was a simple one. We sat down around the table-mat, each of us with a cup and a knife. We ate. We finished. No men came back. No horses came back. When another half-hour had gone by, my husband took Harry and went out to look for them himself.", "The day wore on. One after another they would come in to report progress, which meant no progress. Petaille and Lecuyer eventually turned up with two of the horses. The rest were not anywhere. Mr. Kinzie and Harry came back wet to the knees from the dew standing in the long prairie grass, with nothing to tell us. They ate, and went out again, and came back the same way.", "The rest of us had spent the whole morning at the encampment turning it over. Could the Indians have taken them? It was hardly possible. These people almost never committed a robbery in time of peace, and never against our family, whom they counted among their best friends. The horses would be found. They had probably been carelessly fastened the evening before and had simply wandered farther than they usually did.", "We held a council and decided to send Grignon back to Chicago to get fresh horses from Gholson Kercheval and return as fast as he could. If he found the encampment empty when he got back, he was to understand that we had recovered our own animals and gone on to Fox River, where he would certainly catch us.", "He had been gone less than an hour when the missing horses came hopping slowly out of a point of woods to the north of us — a stand of trees that every one of the searchers swore he had gone over again and again — and made straight for the spot where we were sitting. They came up as demurely as though nothing at all had happened. They seemed genuinely surprised to be met with a hearty scolding instead of the usual patting and praise.", "It took a short half-hour to strike and pack the tent, stow the mats and the kettles, saddle up, and get mounted for the road."],
  p3s4: ["\"Whoever pleases may take my place in the carriage,\" our mother said. \"I have traveled so many years on horseback, that I find any other mode of conveyance too fatiguing.\"", "And then, at sixty years of age, she went up onto sister Margaret's pacer with the ease of a girl of sixteen.", "Lieutenant Foster had gone from us early that morning — he had to rejoin his command — and now the Sau-ga-nash, having seen us ready to start with a clear sky over us and everything right and tight for the trip, said his good-byes and turned back toward Chicago.", "We rode on through a beautiful country, glade and forest by turns, until we came to the Fox River. The current ran clear over its bed, rippling as it went, and as we started down the steep bank toward the water the question came up that always comes up in a country you do not know. Is it fordable?", "Petaille knew this ground. Petaille had not yet caught up to us. Lecuyer knew nothing whatever about it. The troops had plainly gone ahead of us by this same trail — true enough, but the troops had been on horseback. The thing in doubt was whether we could get the carriage over. And understand, the doubt was never about how deep the water was. It was about what lay underneath it, and whether that would hold.", "We agreed that two or three riders should try it first. My mother, Lecuyer, and I went across slowly, each of us picking a different place to come out on the far bank, so as to find the firmest footing. The bottom was hard and good almost the whole way. Near the shore it gave a little under the hooves. Then one more step and each of us stood on dry ground.", "\"Est-il beau?\" my husband called. He was driving.", "\"Oui, monsieur.\"", "\"Yes, John, come just here, it is perfectly good.\"", "\"No, no — go a little farther down. See the white gravel just there — it will be firmer still, there.\"", "That was the sort of direction he was getting, all of it at once and none of it agreeing. He took the second. One step short of the bank, both horses went down until there was hardly more of them showing than their backs.", "The white gravel was not gravel. It was a bed of yellow clay, and the water lying over it had made it gleam like something solid.", "The horses were half suffocated with mud and water and they fought the harness in a frenzy. My husband went out of the seat and onto the pole. \"Some one give me a knife,\" he shouted. I was back in the river before I had decided to be, and I went as close as I dared and put mine into his hand, out of the scabbard I wore around my neck.", "\"Whatever you do, don't cut the traces,\" his mother cried.", "He cut through some of the side-straps. He had worked his way out to the very end of the pole and was reaching forward to get at the head-couplings when one horse gave a wild plunge and the other reared and went over almost onto its back. My husband was between them. For a moment we thought he was gone, trampled down under them. Then he came up out of it, so covered in mud and water that we could barely make him out. Harry was near him, and Harry went forward along the pole with the quickness of a cat, and with the sharp jack-knife he already had open he cut the straps that held their heads.", "By then both horses were lying floating on the surface, one of them to all appearances dead and the other gasping what looked like its last breath. But the instant they felt their heads come free, both of them made another furious effort together to get loose from the pole, which still held them by the neck-strap.", "That failed, so they tried something else. With a few well-judged twists and turns they wrenched the pole apart, and finally carried it off across the river in triumph and up the bank, where they stood together and waited to see what we intended to do next.", "Here was a predicament. A few hours earlier we had thought ourselves badly used because a few of our horses had gone missing. This was worse. The wagon was standing in the river, the harness was cut to pieces and had then been carried off in the most independent manner by Tom and his companion, the pole was twisted into fragments, and there was not so much as a stick on our side of the water to make another one out of.", "Then a whoop came from the far bank, and two or three good ones went back to it from our party, and it was Petaille Grignon, come at last. He got down and took charge of the horses, who were standing under a shady tree resting after their exertions, and by that time Lecuyer had crossed over and the two of them brought the delinquents back.", "Meanwhile we had been doing what we could for our sister Margaret. She had been in the wagon when it went down, with her little son Edwin, and it had taken some work to get the two of them out and carried to shore on horseback. The shock of it brought on a fit of the ague, and she lay now in the long grass of the little prairie along the bank, blue and shaking. The tent had been packed at the back of the wagon and was too soaked with mud and river water to shelter anyone; all we could do was spread it in the sun to dry. We opened an umbrella over her head. Then we started on the question of repairs. First the wagon needed a new pole, which meant the master and the men had to recross the river and pick out an iron-tree in the forest.", "Then the harness. Someone had had the foresight to put a little box under the wagon seat with an awl in it, and waxed ends, and a number of other small things exactly suited to a situation like this one.", "It went question and answer, like Cock Robin:", "\"Who can mend the harness?\"", "\"I can, for I learned when I was a young girl to make shoes as an accomplishment, and I can surely now, as a matter of usefulness and duty, put all those wet, dirty pieces of leather together.\"", "So we all sat down on the grass in the shade of the only two umbrellas we could muster between us.", "I stitched away steadily at it. I will admit that I blistered my hands in no small degree.", "They had brought a suitable young tree back across, and the hatchets — nobody travels in the woods without them — were shaping it into a pole when a peculiar hissing started up somewhere close, and the cry went up at once: \"Un serpent sonnette! A rattlesnake!\"", "Every one of us came to our feet, even the poor shaking invalid, and we were up just in time to see the thing slide past within three inches of my mother's feet, while the men fell on the place it had left with whips and missiles and anything else that would add to the general commotion.", "That little incident turned out to be an excellent cure for the ague. One excitement drives out another, and on that homoeopathic principle sister Margaret came along so well that by the time the day's mischiefs were all repaired she was ready to take her place in the cavalcade, as bright and cheerful as the rest of us."],
  p3s5: ["All of that had cost us so much of the day that our afternoon's ride came to very little. We got no farther than the shore of a beautiful sheet of water that people now call Crystal Lake. Its clear surface was covered over with loons, and with Poules d'Eau — a kind of rail that swarms in this region at certain seasons of the year.", "The Indians have the genius of Aesop for the character of animals. There is not a peculiarity in the look or the habits or the disposition of any creature that they do not have a fable ready to account for.", "The back of the little rail is deeply hollowed, scooped inward. The Indians say it came to be that way like this. Most of the Northwestern tribes hold that there is an invisible being among them, something on the order of the Genie in the stories of the East. Nan-nee-bo-zho is not exactly the father of evil. His office is to punish whatever is amiss, and he is described as forever busy trapping the animals that cross his path and making examples of them.", "One fine evening, walking along the bank of a lake, he saw a flock of ducks out on the blue water, sailing about and enjoying themselves. He called to them.", "\"Ho! come with me into my lodge, and I will teach you to dance!\" Some of the ducks said among themselves, \"It is Nan-nee-bo-zho, let us not go.\" Others thought differently. His words were fair and his voice was easy to listen to, and a few of them turned their faces toward the land. The rest followed soon after, and the whole flock came trooping up the bank behind him with a good deal of pleasant quacking, and went into his lodge.", "Once they were inside, the first thing he did was take up an Indian sack with a wide mouth and tie it by the strings around his neck, so that it hung down over his shoulders with the mouth of it left open. Then he stood himself in the center of the lodge and arranged the ducks in a circle around him.", "\"Now,\" he said, \"you must all shut your eyes tight, whoever opens his eyes at all, something dreadful will happen to him. I will take my Indian flute and play upon it, and you will, at the word I shall give, open your eyes, and begin dancing, as you see me do.\"", "The ducks did as they were told. They shut their eyes tight and kept the time by stepping from one foot to the other, all of them impatient for the dancing to start.", "Then there came a sound like a quack with something over it. None of them dared open their eyes.", "The flute kept breaking off. Each time it broke off there was that gurgling cry again — qu-a-a-ck. There was one little duck among them, much smaller than the others, and at about this point she could not stand it any longer and opened one eye, carefully. She saw Nan-nee-bo-zho playing his flute with one hand, and stooping down every so often to take hold of the duck nearest him, and throttle it, and push it into the bag on his shoulders. She edged herself a little way out of the circle, toward the door, which had been left partly open to let in the light, and then she called out — \"Open your eyes — Nan-nee-bo-zho is choking you all and putting you into his bag!\"", "And with that she flew. Nan-nee-bo-zho came down on her. His hand closed over her back, and she tore herself out of it by main force and got into the open air. Her companions came screaming and quacking out after her. Some of them escaped. Some of them stayed with the sprite.", "The little duck had saved her life and lost her beauty. She kept forever after the shape she had been forced into in that one moment — her back pressed down in the middle, her head and neck stretched out unnaturally into the air."],
  p3s6: ["The third day of our trip came up brilliantly clear, the same as the two before it, and we bent our course farther to the north than we had been going, toward Big-foot Lake — which people now call, with a good deal less imagination, the Lake of Geneva.", "That day nothing went wrong. Nothing at all. The air was soft, the foliage of the forest was fresh and gave off its scent as we passed through it, the little brooks ran clear and bright over their stones, and every part of it spoke the praises of the Creator who had been good to us.", "It is in country like this, a long way off from the noise and the striving and the sin of civilized places, that a person most completely feels the presence of the great Author of the Universe. Here the mind can adore his majesty and his goodness without anything standing in between. Here, and only here, is the command actually kept: Let all the earth keep silence before Him.", "And it can hardly be missed that the deepest and most solemn devotion lives in the hearts of those who are shut out from the worship of God in temples made with hands, and are driven instead to commune with him in the middle of the boundless magnificence that his own power framed.", "The day was not entirely without incident, either. We had stopped for our noon food and drink, and dismounted, and thrown ourselves down on the fresh herbage right at the edge of a pleasant thicket, when a tender bleating started up somewhere close to us. Then the low branches parted and a sweet little dappled fawn came through them and out among us, plainly hunting for its mother. It showed no fear of us whatever. Poor Selkirk's lines might be parodied for it: it was so unacquainted with man, its tameness was charming to us.", "Then the children began shouting with delight, and that sent the fawn bounding straight back into the woods, and any hope of catching it and keeping it for a pet ended right there.", "We had covered good ground that day and were beginning to feel it, when just before sunset we came up onto a ridge that looked down into one of the loveliest little dells I have ever seen. It was an oak opening. Under the shade of the tall scattered trees, the cattle and horses of the soldiers were browsing — they had gotten that far. Two or three white tents stood at the bottom of the valley beside a clear stream. The camp-fires were already lit, and the men were moving about them singly and in groups, seeing to their own comfort or their animals'.", "Lieutenant Foster came out to welcome us with great delight, and accepted without a moment's hesitation an invitation to join our mess again for as long as we should be traveling together.", "We found ourselves a pleasant encamping-ground, far enough off from the other party to spare us any inconvenience, and our supper was improved by a kettle of fine fresh milk brought over by Mrs. Gardiner, the hospital matron, who with her little covered cart was no unimportant feature of that military group. We ate our evening meal with a great deal of hilarity and enjoyment.", "If people are ever good company for one another, it is under circumstances like these. There has always been enough incident in the day to supply the talk and the laughing for as long as anyone is inclined to keep it up — which, to tell the truth, is not an unconscionable length of time after their supper is over.", "The poor Lieutenant looked grave enough when we set out ahead of him the next morning. Nobody in his party knew the road. Mr. Kinzie gave him directions, general and particular, and promised that at every place that looked more than usually doubtful he would blaze a tree or set up a chip to show the way."],
  p3s7: ["The country changed under us now, more varied than anything we had crossed so far — hills swelling up gently, lovely valleys between them, streams running bright at the bottom. What it did not have was living things. Now and then a shout from whoever was leading (we traveled Indian file, as the custom is) would turn all our heads toward a herd of deer loping, as the Westerners say it, off through the trees; or somebody would put a spur in at the sight of a small dark shape far ahead of us on the trail. It never came to anything. The game always contrived to be gone before we could get near it, and to leave the beaten track for a proper hunt was out of the question.", "A little after midday we came down a long sloping knoll, and at a sudden turn the whole lake opened in front of us — Gros-pied to the French, Maunk-suck to the people who lived on it, and Big-foot to us, after the chief whose village stood above its water. Bold hills pushed forward into the clear blue of it, or drew back a little to leave a green level nook where a man might set down a home. Along the nearer shore ran a bright gravelly beach, and here and there a small pure stream came coursing across it to join the larger water.", "On rising ground at the foot of one of the bluffs in the middle distance stood a cluster of neat wigwams, with gardens around them. They did not spoil the picture. They completed it.", "The whole party shouted out loud without meaning to. It was like the Hudson, only less bold — no, it was like the lake of the Forest Cantons in the picture of the Chapel of William Tell. What could be imagined more enchanting? Oh, if our friends at the East could only be here to enjoy it with us.", "We stayed a long while looking at it, and then put the spurs in and skirted the head of the lake, and before long we were climbing the broad platform where the village of Maunk-suck, or Big-foot, stood.", "They had watched us coming from a long way off. Every one of them was assembled in front of the wigwams by the time we arrived, ready to greet us if we were friends, and to do whatever the occasion should call for if we were not. Nothing like the sight of us had ever come up that rise before. Their welcome was every bit as warm as we had expected. \"Shaw-nee-aw-kee\" and his mother, who was known through the whole tribe by that touching name, \"Our friend's wife,\" were received with real kindness, and an animated talk started up that I could follow only as far as the gestures carried it. So I gave myself over to studying everything in front of me.", "The chief was a large man, raw-boned and ugly, his face swollen out of shape by drink, with something sinister and unpleasant in his expression. He had a gay-colored handkerchief tied over his head and was otherwise dressed in his best, out of compliment to the strangers.", "This was the chief to whom Chambly — Shaw-bee-nay, they call him now — and Billy Caldwell and Robinson were sent by Dr. Wolcott, their Agent, during the Winnebago war in 1821, to do everything in their power to keep him and his band from joining the hostile Indians. It took some doing, but they managed it, and by managing it they almost certainly saved the life of every settler then living exposed on the frontier.", "Of all the groups of his people standing around us, none held my eye like one young man. His face was handsome and his figure was striking even in a crowd where every figure was fine and well made. He too had a gay handkerchief on his head, and a shirt of the brightest lemon-colored calico, and silver ornaments in quantity, and — this was what gave the whole costume its strangeness — one legging of blue and the other of bright scarlet. I knew what that particular detail meant. It is the sign of a heart suffering under the tender passion. The flute in his hand said the same thing over again, and the joyous, animated look on his face said, just as plainly, that he was not a lover in any danger of despair.", "It was easy to picture him lately back from the chase, loaded with game, walking into the lodge of the fair one as the custom is and throwing his burden down at her parents' feet with a superb, indifferent air, as much as to say: here is some meat, it is a mere trifle, but it will show you what you might expect with me for a son-in-law. And I had no doubt at all that the girl had stepped forward and gathered it up, which is how she accepts the offering and the man who brought it. There was nothing in the look or the manner of any of the young women around us to show which of them was the happy one, and though I searched every face I could not find so much as a conscious blush anywhere. So I picked out the youngest and the prettiest of them for myself and went on weaving my romance to my own satisfaction.", "The village sat inside a ring of hills so steep, cut by gorges so narrow, that climbing out seemed barely possible on horseback. How we were to get a four-wheeled carriage up was the question now being argued between my husband and the Pottowattamies. There was no choice but to try it, by whichever pass the inhabitants judged the most practicable. Petaille went first and I came after him on my favorite Jerry. It was a scramble of a kind one does not often take — very nearly straight up, through what looked like the dry bed of a torrent, filled now with loose stones and hardly offering one secure foothold from the bottom to the top. I hung on to the mane, and part of the way I had both arms around Jerry's neck outright, and with the shouts and cheers of the people below coming up after us we got there — safe, and very nearly out of breath — and sat on the pinnacle looking down to see how the next party would fare.", "The horses had been taken out of the carriage. Its luggage went up on the shoulders of the young Indians, to be toted to the top. Then ropes were made fast to its sides and a whole crowd of our red friends took hold of them, with our two Frenchmen at the head, and two or three more got in behind to hold it and keep it from running backward. Then the work started. Such pulling. Such shouting. Such clapping of hands from the spectators of both sexes, and such stentorian orders and encouragement bawled out by the bourgeois. Every so often there would be a check, a wavering, the whole thing seeming about to go over backward into the plain below — and then no, they would have it again, and after efforts I would not have believed possible the carriage came safely up onto the table-land with the rest of us. In time everything and everyone was landed there. We paid our friends to their satisfaction, gathered up the goods and chattels, repacked the wagon, and set off for our encampment at Turtle Creek.", "Between the labor of that climb, the excitement of it, and a sun that kept getting hotter, that afternoon's ride was more uncomfortable than anything we had felt on the whole journey. We were truly glad when our guide's whoop went up and a few scattered lodges came into view and we knew we had reached our ground. We picked a beautiful sequestered spot beside a clear, sparkling stream, and when we had dismounted and seen the horses made comfortable, my husband gave his men their directions and then took me off to a private place where I could put aside my hat and my mask and bathe my flushed face and aching head in the cool water. I have never felt anything so grateful, so delicious. I sat down and leaned my head back against one of the tall trees overshadowing the place, and I was nearly dreaming when they called me in to the evening meal.", "The Indians had made us a present of some fine brook trout, and our Frenchmen had cooked them in the most tempting way, and before the bright moon came up and we were ready for our rest, the headache and the fatigue had both gone out of me.", "One of the finest things about traveling this way is the joyous, vocal life of the forest at first light, when every feathered creature in it comes out to pay its cheerful salutation to the opening day."],
  p3s8: ["The bob-o'-link runs off his rapid chattering flourish; the thrush lays a soft whistle over it; the wood-dove puts in her tender coo, and the grouse a deep warbling bass, and the partridge his drumming; then the lark's melodious trill, the robin's gay carol, and the friendly, familiar calling of the duck and the teal — and all of it coming at once out of the trees and the knolls and the low ground, until the simple half-breed says the only thing there is to say: \"Voilà la forêt qui parle!\" It seems as though a man could not help raising his own voice to take his part in the chorus — the mating song of praise.", "Birds and flowers and the soft balmy air of the morning. Must it not have been in a scene like this one that Milton's Adam poured out that beautiful hymn of adoration — These are thy glorious works, Parent of Good?", "Our hope for that day was to reach, early, the broad expanse of the Rock River that widens here into the Kosh-ko-nong. What that name means, in a language whose people's later fate makes it doubly affecting to say, is the lake we live on.", "For the first part of the day the road ran through forest so thick and tangled that Grignon and Lecuyer had to go out ahead of us as pioneers with their axes, cutting away the shrubs and branches that shut the way. It was slow work and at times it was discouraging. We got through it at last, and came out into country of an entirely different kind — low prairie, cut across by deep narrow streams as straight as canals, and every one of them a delay for horses and a real difficulty for carriages.", "Several times that forenoon the horses had to be taken out of the carriage and the carriage pulled and pushed over those deep narrow channels however it could be managed.", "Nobody has ever been happier to see the wooded banks of the Kosh-ko-nong than we were when they finally came up in front of us. Five or six miles of riding through beautiful oak openings brought us to Man-Eater's village — a cluster of neat bark wigwams with wide fields on either side of it, corn and beans and squashes lately planted and already promising a fine crop. In front of it lay the broad blue lake. To the south its shores were open and marshy; near the village, and away north as far as we could see, they were lined with fine tall trees. The village stood a little below the point where the Rock River opens into the lake, and while our party talked with the Indians there, an arrangement was made for them to take us across at a place about half a mile above.", "After a short halt we took up the line of march again through the woods, following the bank of the river.", "A number of the Winnebagoes went by water toward the appointed place, paddling the largest and strongest of their canoes — we had been among our own people ever since we left Gros-pied Lake.", "At the spot we dismounted and the men began unsaddling and unloading. We were put into the canoes first and paddled over to the far bank. Then the horses were swum across. Then the carriage. Two long wooden canoes were lashed firmly together side by side, wide enough between them for the carriage to stand upright, and the crossing began. Again and again those tottering hulls would lean, and a cry or a shout would go up from the party on shore as the whole mass looked to be going over sideways into the river — and then it would right itself, and after a good many departures from the perpendicular it came to shore safely.", "Now we thought our troubles were finished and there was nothing left to do but mount up and trot for Fort Winnebago as fast as we could go. No. Half a mile on there was a swamp. It was not wide, that much is true. It was two to three feet deep in mud and water, and whether we could get the carriage across it at all was an open question. Several of the Indians came along with us to that place, partly to lend their help and advice, and partly for the pleasure of watching what happened.", "When we got to it, we were inclined to laugh at all the formidable things that had been said about it. What we saw was a strip of somewhat low ground with tall dry rushes standing all over it.", "The ground did look a little wet. There was nothing there to justify the alarm anyone had been raising. So it was a considerable surprise to me when my husband, who had been gone from us a few minutes, came back into the circle wearing his duck trousers, with no shoes and no stockings on.", "\"What are you going to do?\" asked I.", "\"Carry you through the swamp on my shoulders. Come, Petaille, you are the strongest — you are to carry Madame Kinzie, and To-shun-nuck there,\" pointing to a tall, stout Winnebago, \"he will take Madame Helm.\"", "\"Wait a moment,\" I said, and I sat down on the grass and took off my own boots and stockings without hurrying about it.", "\"What is that for?\" they all asked.", "\"Because I don't wish to ride with wet feet all the rest of the day.\"", "\"No danger of that,\" they said, and not one of them did as I had done.", "By the time they were out in the middle of it, they had reason to think better of my precaution. The water our bearers had to wade was deep enough that nothing the ladies could do kept their feet above the surface of it, and I had the satisfaction of knowing that my weight on my husband's shoulders was far less trouble to him than it might have been, since I could hold the position I had started in instead of shifting about constantly to stay clear of the water.", "The laugh was entirely on my side when I put my boots and stockings back on and got up into my saddle dry-shod.", "It will be seen that traveling in the woods is, to some extent, a deranger of ceremony and formality, and that it necessarily cuts into our conventionalities. The only remedy for it is to take a double share of them when we come back to the civilized walks of life.", "By dint of a great deal of pulling and shouting and encouraging and threatening, the horses finally dragged the carriage through that ugly passage, and our red friends were free to go back to their village with what was certainly a very exaggerated and very amusing account of everything they had seen and had a hand in.", "We had not forgotten our promise to Lieutenant Foster about a guide-board of some kind, to help him follow us. Several times we carried pieces out of the woods with us, three or four feet long, and planted them at points where the way was uncertain, with a stick set crosswise through a cleft in the top to show which direction he and his party were to take.", "We felt sure that, coming a few days behind us, he would find our trail and have the benefit of the same help we had had in getting through the hard places."],
  p3s9: ["That night we were to camp somewhere short of the Four Lakes. The heat had taken everything out of us, and the day's riding had taken the rest, and we watched the ground ahead for the low clumps of willow and alder that meant water was under them. None of us had another hour in us. We could have pushed on to the bank of the nearest lake, but the lake lay off our line, and off our line was a luxury we could not afford.", "It was late when we found a place that would do. We told each other, more than once, that this was the last night we would sleep on the road. Tomorrow we would be at Winnebago.", "The whole trip had been a pleasure — one long stretch of high spirits, and even the mishaps, which had been maddening while they lasted, had turned into things we laughed about afterward. Still, people are made the way they are made. We were ready to trade all that excitement for a quiet room and a door that shut.", "The next morning's ride was calmer than any that had gone before it, because early in the day we came out onto what everyone called the Twenty-mile Prairie, and I will say here that miles on the prairie are longer than miles anywhere else. Crossing it was like crossing a desert with the sand taken out. Mile after mile of open ground, unbroken. Not a tree. Not one living thing in sight except ourselves.", "The sun seemed to be making up for two months of hiding, and it burned twice as hard for the arrears. You cannot carry an umbrella on horseback. The ones in the wagon could, and did.", "Our mother's strength had held her in the saddle every day until this one. On this one it simply ran out, and she gave up her place on little Brunet to sister Margaret.", "So we rode, one low knoll lifting past another, and from the top of each of them in turn we looked for the far line of timber that was, to us, the promised land.", "\"Take courage,\" came the encouraging word, again and again, \"very soon you will begin to see the timber.\"", "Another hour would drag itself past.", "\"Now, when we reach the rising ground just ahead, look sharp.\"", "We looked sharp. The same landscape, unvarying, out to every edge of the world.", "There were not even streams out there to touch the thirst that fatigue and impatience had built in us.", "Then a whoop from Shaw-nee-aw-kee broke the silence we had been riding in.", "\"Le voilà!\" (There it is!)", "Our unpractised eyes could not find it at first — a faint blue strip laid along the horizon — and then we could, and it grew, and went on growing, and as it grew the fatigue and the thirst and the misery went out of us in exact proportion.", "We were in high spirits by the time we came in under Hastings's Woods, a fine, tall forest with a clear, quick stream running through it.", "The green shade and the cold water were food and drink to us, but we did not let ourselves forget that the day was moving on, and that we meant to be in before sunset. So we made ourselves get up and go, when another hour under those trees would have been the easiest thing in the world.", "The marsh at Duck Creek, after a week of that heat, was nothing like what it had been a few months earlier, when I had gone down into its ice water.", "We crossed it without trouble and came out on the bank of the creek itself.", "The stream was held to be fordable at that point, and in any case most of our party would not have cared much either way. To the ladies the question seemed to want a little more attention than that.", "\"This water looks very deep — are you sure we can cross it on horseback?\"", "\"Oh, yes! Petaille, go before, and let us see how the water is.\"", "Petaille went. He was mounted on a horse built like a giraffe, and by sticking his legs straight out to either side he got across with hardly a splash on him.", "The water was plainly going to come into the wagon. That mattered less, since at worst the passengers could climb up and stand on the seats.", "My horse, Jerry, was a good deal taller than average, so I was over quickly, with no trouble beyond having to pull my feet out of the stirrups and tuck them up tight against his mane.", "Sister Margaret was still on Brunet. Somebody said she had better change to one of the taller horses, and while that was being discussed the perverse little wretch settled it himself by walking into the creek after the others, without consulting anyone.", "He was out of his depth almost at once. We were frightened and delighted at the same moment, watching her draw her feet up out of the stirrups with astonishing quickness and perch herself on the very top of the saddle, where she stayed, and steered her small mutinous horse safely up onto the far bank.", "That was the last of our adventures. Four easy miles brought us to the Fort with the sun laying its last light across the whole glowing country, and at the ferry the friends who had been waiting for us took us straight up to the hospitable roof of Major Twiggs."],
  p3s10: ["The companies of the First Infantry that had been holding Fort Winnebago had their orders before we ever rode in. They were to move on to the Mississippi the moment a portion of the Fifth, then at Fort Howard, came up to relieve them.", "A good many of the officers of the Fifth had wives, which meant every set of quarters in the post would be spoken for. So although Major Twiggs pressed us hard to come back inside the Fort and stay until he went on furlough, we decided it was better to set ourselves up at once at \"the Agency.\"", "It was a very grand name for a very small concern, and I could never say it without wanting to laugh. The heads of department at Washington had promised us a comfortable house as soon as Congress voted money enough to cover any extra expense in the Indian Department. Congress, it was clear, bore us some private grudge, because it had let two whole sessions go by without troubling itself about where we slept. There was nothing to be done about that. We would be comfortable on whatever we had.", "What we had were the old log barracks, thrown up two years before for the officers and men when the post was first established. Our French engagés had taken them apart and stood them up again on the little hill across from the Fort. To these were now added a dairy, stables, a smoke-house and the rest, built of tamarack logs hauled out of the swamp nearby. The whole arrangement looked exactly as rough and as makeshift as it was.", "The main building was a row of four rooms. No two of them opened into each other. Each had its own door onto the open air, and a small window cut straight through the logs front and back to let in light. An enormous clay chimney served every two rooms and took up one whole side of each. Overhead, where a ceiling should have been, there were a few rough boards laid across the transverse logs that carried the roof.", "It was surprising how quickly those old broken-down rooms began to feel like a home — a few Indian mats down on the floor, the piano and the rest of the furniture set where furniture belongs, even some pictures hung against the logs. The pictures did not last. With the first hard rain the water found its way in through any number of crevices, and after that we made a habit of putting away anything that moisture could ruin. We laughed about it. We also packed the carpets and the extra furniture up onto the boards overhead, which we grandly called the attic, and settled down to wait for the day the Government would condescend to remember that we existed. The thing that wore on me most was the straw bonnet. I had to wear it all day long, crossing from bedroom to parlor and from parlor to kitchen, and I got so used to the weight of it that I would sometimes forget to take it off when I sat down to the table, or to some quiet piece of work with my mother and sister."],
  p3s11: ["Permission did come, in time, to build a house for the blacksmith — meaning the man the Government kept in pay at this station to mend the guns and traps of the Indians.", "It happened to be the luckiest thing possible for us that Monsieur Isidore Morrin was a bachelor, and perfectly content to go on boarding with his friend Louis Frum, dit Manaigre. So once the new house was genuinely under way, we laid it out and drove it forward strictly on our own account.", "It was no palace. A parlor and two bedrooms on the ground floor, two low chambers up under the roof, a kitchen at the back. Set beside the rambling stable of a building we were living in then, it might as well have been one.", "Before it was finished, word reached Mr. Kinzie that the money for the annual Indian payment was waiting for him in Detroit — his to take charge of, his to see safely carried to the Portage — and he had to leave at once.", "The workmen brought up from the Mississippi to put up the main building were good enough to go on without anyone standing over them. The kitchen, though, was the Frenchmen's job, and nobody could say how that would get done with the bourgeois gone.", "\"You will have to content yourselves in the old quarters until my return,\" said my husband, \"and then we will soon have things in order.\"", "His trip would be a long, slow business. The Government did not move by railroad and telegraph in those days.", "After he was gone I said to the men, \"Come, you have all your logs cut and hauled — the squaws have brought the bark for the roof — what is to prevent our finishing the house and getting all moved and settled to surprise Monsieur John on his return?\"", "\"Ah! to be sure, Madame John,\" said Plante, who always did the talking, \"provided the one who plants a green bough on the chimney-top is to have a treat.\"", "\"Certainly. All hands fall to work, and see who will win the treat.\"", "On the strength of a prize for whoever laid the last stroke on the building, Plante, Pillon, and Manaigre — whom the waggish Plante insisted on calling \"mon nègre,\" always at a moment when he was safely out of arm's reach — went at it hard.", "Building a log house is a strange process to watch. The logs go up one on another, jointed at the corners, until the walls stand as high as they are meant to stand. The chimney starts as four poles of the right length, laced together with a wicker-work of small branches. Someone digs a pit close by and works clay and water into a kind of mortar. Big wisps of hay are loaded with the thick stuff and shaped by hand into what are called, in the trade, clay cats, and these get packed into the frame of the chimney until there is not a chink left anywhere in it. Then the whole thing is smoothed over with a coat of wet clay, and that is what they call plastering.", "Between the logs of the walls, small bits of wood are driven in close together — that is chinking — and once it is done the clay cats go in after them and are smoothed down with the plaster. When it has all dried, the walls and the chimney are whitewashed, and the result looks tidy and comfortable, and nothing at all like the pit it came out of.", "The roof is thick sheets of bark laid over the transverse logs. Around the chimney, where rain does its worst, we were careful to have a few layers of palisade put down — the ones left over when Mr. Peach, an odd little itinerant genius, had fenced in our garden, which was the pride and the wonder of every settlement and wigwam for miles around."],
  p3s12: ["All the while this work was going on, our Indian friends kept coming to see us. First among them, and loudest, was the young Dandy — Four-Legs.", "He turned up one fine morning with two women, whom he presented as his wives. He had a little Chippewa, and on that thin bridge he and our mother contrived to hold something like a conversation. He had come in everything he owned: brooches, wampum, fan, looking-glass, the whole inventory. The paint on his face and across his chest had plainly taken him a long while to get right.", "He took a chair, the way he had seen it done in Washington, and signed to his women to sit down on the floor.", "Taking two wives is not a common thing among the Indians. They appear to have worked out that the fewer of them a man has to manage, the more peace there is in the wigwam.", "Still, it happens now and then that a husband gets a foolish notion about a second woman, and then he has to spend every scrap of cunning and eloquence he owns bringing the first one around to the idea of another body in the lodge. It has to be handled delicately, or there will be no harmony in that house again.", "\"My dear, your health isn't very good, it is time you should have some rest. You have worked very hard, and it grieves me that you should have to labor any longer. Let me get you some nice young squaw to wait upon you, that you may live at ease all the rest of your life.\"", "The first wife agrees. She has no choice about it. And if she happens to be a jealous woman with a long memory, the life the newcomer leads after that does not bear describing. The old wife keeps every right she has as dowager and duenna, and the husband's tenderness is thin payment for what the young rival is put through.", "The Dandy came to us on a Sunday morning. We were all sitting quietly, reading. Four-Legs asked my mother why we were doing that, and why everything around us was so still.", "My mother explained the day of rest to him — that we set it aside for worshipping and serving the Great Spirit, as he had commanded in his Holy Word.", "Four-Legs nodded his approval. That was quite right, he said. He was glad to see us doing our duty. He was very religious himself, and he liked to see it in other people. He always saw to it that his squaws attended to their duties too — not reading, perhaps, but the sort of thing the Great Spirit liked, and that he considered proper and becoming.", "He had no appetite at all for hearing where our beliefs and his parted company. The conviction among the Winnebagoes that if the Great Spirit had wanted them any different from what they are, he would have made them so, is too solid a thing for argument or persuasion to move.", "Sometimes a man who wants to appear a little civilized will sit through everything you have to say about Christianity, and then remark pleasantly, \"Yes, we believe that too,\" and turn the talk to something else.", "As a rule they do not see what there is to be gained by taking up the religion and the habits of the whites. \"Look at them,\" they say, \"always toiling and striving — always wearing a brow of care — shut up in houses — afraid of the wind and the rain — suffering when they are deprived of the comforts of life! We, on the contrary, live a life of freedom and happiness. We hunt and fish, and pass our time pleasantly in the open woods and prairies. If we are hungry, we take some game, or, if we don't find that, we can go without. If our enemies trouble us, we can kill them, and there is no more said about it. What should we gain by changing ourselves into white men?\""],
  p3s13: ["Whatever the Christian missionaries had done, and they had worked hard at it, they had made very little headway in lighting up anybody's mind on the doctrines of the Gospel. While we were living at the Portage, Mr. Mazzuchelli, a Roman Catholic priest, came up on a missionary visit with Miss Elizabeth Grignon along as interpreter, and after some instruction from him about forty of them agreed to be baptized. They were given Christian names, which pleased them very much, and little plated crucifixes, which pleased them more. The women wore them around their necks. There was real devotion in the way they handled those crosses, but I had not enough of the language to learn from any of them whether they understood the first thing about what the symbol was meant to carry. This much is certain. Not one of them asked to learn our language so as to know the Saviour better, and not one of them showed the least anxiety to be told anything more about him than the missionary had managed to say in his short stay.", "One of the women, who had been given the name Charlotte, said she wanted to learn how white women kept house, and asked me, through Madame Paquette, whether she might come on washing-day and learn our way of doing the business from my servants. We gave her a tub, and my woman showed her by signs and example what she was to do. I was more than a little curious to see how it was going, so after a while I went out to the kitchen where they all were. Charlotte was standing at her tub, scouring and rubbing away with everything in her at her little crucifix. Two other women sat on the floor beside her, watching her do it.", "\"That is the work she has been at for the last half-hour,\" said Josette, out of all patience. \"She'll never learn to wash.\"", "Charlotte did then fall to in earnest, and went at the washing so hard it looked as though she meant to pull her own arms out of their sockets.", "After a time I decided she must be worn out with work she was not used to, and I did what everyone did in those days — what I had always seen done at home on washing-day, what I imagine was simply what housekeepers did. I went to the dining-room closet meaning to bring her a glass of wine, or brandy and water. My cupboard turned out to be in the condition of poor Mother Hubbard's. There was nothing of the kind in it anywhere, only a bottle of orange shrub.", "I poured out a wineglassful of that and carried it to her. She took it with obvious pleasure. Then, with it already on the way to her lips, she stopped. She said one word — \"Whiskey!\" — and handed it straight back to me. I would have pressed it on her even so, because in my ignorance I honestly believed a cordial was what she needed. She pointed to her crucifix, shook her head, and went back to her tub.", "I took that as a lesson worth more than twenty sermons. It was the first time in my life I had seen anyone refuse spirituous liquor on a religious principle, and it left a mark on me that I have never lost."],
  p3s14: ["Of all the women of the tribe we came to know in those first months, our favorite was a daughter of one of the Day-kau-rays. That family, as I have said elsewhere, claimed a cross of French blood somewhere back up the line, which may explain her fair skin and the soft curl in her hair. She had a fine broad forehead, full expressive eyes, good teeth. She had not gone brown and haggard with age the way the women of her people usually did. Except for one feature, you would have called her beautiful.", "Years before, she had married a Mus-qua-kee, a Fox Indian, and by the custom that holds among all the tribes it was the husband who came home to the wife's family, so he had been living among the Winnebagoes.", "It is this custom, the exact reverse of ours, that makes the birth of a daughter something to be congratulated on in an Indian family. She will bring another hunter to our lodge — that is how they say it to one another.", "The Mus-qua-kee stayed on among his wife's relations for a few years. No children came, and after a while he had had enough of these new people of his and wanted to go back to his own. For a time he worked on his wife to leave her home and go with him to the Mississippi, where the Sauks and Foxes lived. She would not. She could not make herself do it.", "One day, after a great many attempts that came to nothing, he lost his temper.", "\"Then, if you won't go with me,\" he said, \"I will leave you, but you shall never be the wife of any other man — I will mark you!\"", "And then he was on her, and he bit off the end of her nose. That is the usual punishment for infidelity in a wife, and it is the worst disgrace she can carry, because it shuts her out of marriage for the rest of her life. He ran off to his own people afterward. His revenge did not take, though. Day-kau-ray was too widely known and too thoroughly respected for any shame to stick to a member of his family, and this bright, warm creature in particular won everyone she met on the first meeting. She certainly had us from the start.", "She suffered badly from rheumatism, and a remedy we gave her relieved her almost entirely, after which her gratitude had no bottom to it. Long pain had left her partly crippled, but that did not stop her walking the whole ten miles from the Barribault as often as once every two or three weeks just to see us. Then she would sit and look at us, and laugh out loud with a child's delight at anything new or strange we happened to be doing, and pat us and stroke us whenever we came within reach, and sometimes lift one of our hands or arms and kiss it. That was how she showed love. We loved her back. It was always a happy shout in that house when somebody looking out along the Portage road called, \"The Cut-Nose is coming!\" In time, though, we learned to call her by her baptismal name, Elizabeth, because she too was one of Mr. Mazzuchelli's converts."],
  p3s15: ["She came one day with a half-grown boy behind her, and the boy was carrying a young fawn she had brought me for a present. I was charmed with it — the soft eyes, the dappled coat — but I had heard people say \"as wild as a fawn\" all my life and I did not expect to get anywhere with taming it. To my complete surprise, it learned in no time to follow me like a dog. Wherever I went, Fan went. At breakfast she would lie down under the table across my feet. One of her earliest gestures of affection was to chew every bit of trimming off my black silk apron while pretending to nuzzle and fuss over me. That was not the only kind of mischief in her, either.", "One day there was a great clattering of crockery from the kitchen. We ran in to see what had happened and found that Miss Fan had climbed onto a shelf of the dresser, about two feet off the floor, and was working out how to lie down comfortably among the plates and dishes. It did not take me long to see that what she wanted was the shelf above her head — that she always looked for a place to sleep under a chair, or under anything that made a roof of sorts over her. So after that, when her morning nap was coming on, I would open a big green parasol and stand it on the matting in the corner, and when I called \"Fan, Fan,\" she would come and settle in under it and go straight to sleep.", "One morning Fan was gone. We called for her and hunted the garden, the cattle enclosure, the Frenchmen's houses, the whole length of the hill toward Paquette's. No Fan anywhere. We decided her own wild nature had finally spoken and she had gone off to the woods.", "It was a hot forenoon and every door in the house stood open. Around dinner-time Fan came in at a dead run, panting so hard her whole body worked with it, and threw herself down on her side with her legs stretched straight out and her mouth foaming, and everything about her said she was dying. We tried to give her water. We tried to gentle her, in case it was only fright that had her like that. In a few minutes she gave a gasp, and a spasm went through her, and she was dead. Whether the greyhounds had run her, or whether she had eaten some poisonous weed and the pain of it had sent her back to the people she trusted most, we never found out. We only knew we had lost our pretty pet, and a good many tears were shed over her."],
  p3s16: ["Not long at all after my husband rode out, we had a visit from the White Crow, the Little Priest, and several more of the principal chiefs of the Rock River Indians. It plainly threw them to learn that their Father was away from home, even when they were told his errand was to go and get the silver. We sent for Paquette, and he interpreted for us what they had come about.", "They had come to tell us that the Sauk chief Black Hawk and his band — who under an earlier treaty had gone across and settled west of the Mississippi some time before — were back on their old homes and hunting-grounds. They had said plainly that they did not intend to give them up again, and that they meant to drive off the white settlers who had begun to move onto them.", "The driving off, in fact, the chief had already done. It was said he had brought some of the Pottowattamies over to him, and there was reason to be afraid he could talk some of the Winnebagoes into doing the same.", "The chiefs had come to take counsel with their Father, and to give him their word that they would do everything in their power to keep their young men quiet. They had heard that troops were being raised down among the whites in Illinois, and they hoped their own people would have the sense to stay clear of the trouble. And they asked that their Father, when he came back, would see to it that the soldiers did not meddle with them, so long as they stayed quiet and behaved in a friendly way.", "White Crow seemed particularly set on making me understand one thing: that if any danger came while Shaw-nee-aw-kee was gone, he would come with his people and protect me and my family. I believed him. He had never in anything shown himself other than an upright and honorable man.", "Even so. Indian troubles that close, and our guardian and protector somewhere out on the lakes, made for a great many hours in which none of us said much. It was not until word came that the Sauks and Foxes had gone back peaceably west of the Mississippi that we could put the fear down altogether."],
  p3s17: ["Then came the parting with the Twiggses, and I found I could not manage it lightly. We had leaned on that household all winter. Before the last of the goodbyes the Major drew me aside. He had one final report to deliver on our old acquaintance, Krissman.", "\"When I went into the barracks the other day,\" he said, \"about the time the men were taking their dinner, I noticed a great six-foot soldier standing against the window-frame, crying and blubbering. 'Halloo,' I said, 'what on earth does this mean?'", "\"'Why, that fellow there,' said Krissman (for it was he), 'has scrowged me out of my place!' A pretty soldier your protége will make, madam!\" added the Major.", "That was the last word I ever had of my hero. Perhaps he carried those six feet of grievance south and displayed his prowess to the Seminoles and the Mexicans. Perhaps he went back instead to the fat soil of his native German Flats, and to the boatman's horn he so loved to blow. Some historian yet unborn will have to settle it.", "One person is still to be accounted for: Louisa. A chance offered in the spring, and the Major put her into the keeping of a traveller bound for Buffalo, to be delivered home to her parents. She left in style. Out of compliment to the new acquaintances she had formed, she cut her skirts short and drew on a pair of scarlet leggings worked over with porcupine-quills, and so made her farewell to military life. Into the gentleman's hands she also placed sixty dollars for safe keeping, money she said \"she had saved up, out of her wages at a dollar a week, through the winter.\""],
  p3s18: ["We were barely settled in the new house at the Agency before we tried starting a little Sunday-school. Edwin, Harry and Josette were the scholars we could count on, but there were also the two small Manaigres, Thérèse Paquette, and Thérèse's mother's half-sister, Florence Courville, a pretty girl of fifteen. Not one of the girls had ever learned her letters. They spoke only French, or rather the Canadian patois, and it was terribly hard work to give them the sound of a word and its meaning at the same time, and they always wanted the meaning. On top of that there was the business of undoing false ideas, and of clearing away the ignorance and superstition that stood in the road of any sensible improvement. We did what we could, and we had the satisfaction of seeing them, after a while, get on quite respectably with the spelling-book — and, better than that, come by a little light and knowledge about better things.", "In time, though, Florence began to be missing from her class. Her sister, she said, could not always spare her. She wanted her to keep house while she herself went over on Sunday to visit her friends the Roys, who lived on the Wisconsin.", "We took the matter up with Madame Paquette. Could she not spare Florence for one hour of the day? We would gladly teach her on a week-day, since she seemed anxious to learn — but we had always been told that for that there was no time.", "\"Well — she would see. Madame Alum\" — she meant Helm — \"and Madame John were so kind!\"", "The regularity never improved. After a while Manaigre was persuaded to send his children down to Mr. Cadle's mission-school at Green Bay. Thérèse went with them, and very soon after that Florence stopped coming at all.", "From then on we had to keep our teaching inside our own household."],
  p3s19: ["Long before we had any business expecting my husband back, a message came down asking me to come up to the new house. We all went together. We had kept away on purpose for a few days, waiting for exactly that summons, and we knew what it meant.", "Plante was up on the roof beside the kitchen chimney, sitting astride a small keg and enormously pleased with himself. On the very top of the chimney he had planted a green bough. He held on to it with one hand and waved the other at us and shouted, \"Eh bien, Madame John! à cette heure, pour le régal!\"", "\"Yes, Plante, you are entitled to a treat, and I hope you won't enjoy it the less that Pillon and Manaigre are to share it with you.\"", "A suitable reward left all three of them perfectly satisfied with their bourgeoise — against whom Plante had been known to grumble, on the grounds, he said, that she had him called up too early in the morning. He might have added that she could not follow the philosophy by which he came in to work in his own garden on the plea that it was too rainy to work in Monsieur John's.", "It was no ordinary feeling with which we walked out of the old log tenement and took possession of the new house, small and unimportant a thing as it was.", "I was only too glad of the luxury of a real bedchamber after more than two months of sleeping on the parlor floor. Our cooking arrangements, I admit, had not risen much. The clay chimney was not strong enough to carry the trammel and pot-hooks, which in those days had not yet been shoved aside by the cooking-stove and the kitchen-range. We built the fire the old way — great logs at the back, smaller sticks in front laid across the andirons — and any pot that would not fit on the hearth was set up on those sticks. But let one treacherous stick burn through while nobody was watching it, and down came the whole arrangement: kettles, saucepans, burning brands and cinders in one mass you could hardly separate. How often that happened while Harry or little Josette had charge of it, and the mistress of the house was in the parlor playing lady to a visitor, it were vain to tell.", "Then, for all of Monsieur Plante's palisades around the chimney, a hard shower would come pelting down, and the hearth sloped just enough the wrong way that the fire would drown. The bark on the roof did not always do its duty either, and now and then we were so thoroughly rained on indoors that there was nothing for it but to snatch up the breakfast or the dinner and tuck it away under the table until better times — that is, until it was fair again. In spite of every one of these small hostilities, we liked our new quarters enormously.", "The garden was doing well and full of vegetables, and even the currant bushes we had carried up from Chicago, tied in a bundle on the back of the carriage, had given us a little fruit.", "The Indian women came steadily, and they never came without presents. Sometimes venison, sometimes ducks or pigeons, whortleberries, wild plums or cranberries as the season turned, neat pretty mats for the floor or the table, wooden bowls and ladles, fancy work in deer-skin or porcupine-quills. They would carry these in and drop them at my feet. If I was distracted and failed to look pleased, and to lift the things up off the floor and put them carefully away, there would be a look of real hurt, and the remark, \"Our mother hates our gifts,\" and I would know exactly how much I had wounded them. A present had to be received graciously, and it had to be returned with something worth twice as much.", "Meanwhile the weeks went by, one after another, and still the master did not come.", "Schooners reached Green Bay so seldom that no one could ever say how long a passage across the lakes and back would take, and it was the end of August before he came home. He had not expected to find us moved and settled in and doing nicely, and it astonished him. With him there to drive it, the troubles we had been complaining of were put right in short order.", "At Fort Gratiot he had met my young brother Julian, and brought him along — my parents were sending him out, at our asking, to live with us. Edwin was overjoyed to have a companion again, having been very much alone until then. The boys had plenty to look at soon enough, because the summons had gone out to the villages, and the Indians began coming in for the payment."],
  p3s20: ["There was one among them that year I had never laid eyes on before — the mother of the elder Day-kau-ray. Nobody could tell you her age. Everyone agreed she had seen upwards of a hundred winters. Her eyes had gone dim and nearly white. Her face was dark and shrunken in on itself like a baked apple. Her voice shook and had almost nothing left in it, except when she raised it in fury at her graceless grandsons, who liked to play every kind of trick on her. All of it said the same thing about how long she had been alive.", "She went about on all fours, having no strength left to hold herself upright. On the day of the payment, once she had her portion and had hidden it away in a corner of her blanket, she came crawling along and settled herself on the door-step to count what she had.", "My sister and I were watching her from the open window.", "She had just spread the silver out in front of her, unobserved, as she believed, when two of her descendants came down on her out of nowhere. At first they seemed to be begging a share of it. She drove them off with angry gestures. Then one of them made a fast swoop with his hand and came away with a fistful.", "She tried to get up and go after him and could do nothing but clutch what was left and let out screams of rage that did not sound like anything human. Just then the boys happened to look up and see us watching them. They burst out laughing, and with a sort of mocking flourish they threw the half-dollars back to her and ran off to the pay-ground.", "For all their tormenting she was extremely fond of them, and she never came without begging something of her Father that she could turn around and give to them.", "She crept into the parlor one morning, then hauled herself straight up by the frame of the door and cried, in the most pitiful voice, \"Shaw-nee-aw-kee! Wau-tshob-ee-rah Thsoonsh-koo-nee-noh!\" (Silver-man, I have no looking-glass.) My husband, smiling, picked up the same small tone and called back, \"Do you wish to look at yourself, mother?\"", "She found this so irresistibly funny that she laughed until she had to sit herself down on the floor and give herself over to it. Then she admitted the mirror was for one of the boys. When her Father had handed it over, she discovered that she had no comb. Then that she had no knife. Then that she had no calico shawl — until it finished the way it always finished, with Shaw-nee-aw-kee paying pretty dearly for his joke."],
  p3s21: ["Watching the Indians come in, and watching them go out again, did more damage to my sense of woman's rights than anything else I saw in that country. As a rule the master of the family came along at his leisure, carrying his gun and perhaps a lance. The woman came behind him with the mats and the poles of the whole lodge on her shoulders, her pappoose if she had one, her kettles, her sacks of corn and wild rice, and not seldom the family dog riding on the top of the load. If there happened to be a horse or a pony among the family's possessions, the man rode it and the squaw walked after.", "This lopsided division of the work does not come from any lack of kind or affectionate feeling in the husband. It is more the instinct of the sex to assert its superior standing and importance whenever a decent opportunity to do it comes along. Away from anybody's eyes, where there is no danger to his dignity, the husband is willing enough to take some of the burden custom puts on his wife, and to carry a share of her labors and her hardships himself.", "The payment had not gone by without its proper number of complimentary dances and medicine dances. The medicine dance comes only at long intervals. The complimentary dance comes whenever an occasion turns up that calls for a show of respect and courtesy.", "The custom is to go first to the person to be complimented and ask leave to dance for him. Leave granted, the preparation begins: the face painted with great care, the body marked — it is generally bare across the chest and shoulders — after whatever pattern is most approved. Every ornament that can be got hold of goes into the hair or the headdress. And happy is the man who, having taken a scalp or more than one, has the right to announce it with the matching number of eagle's feathers.", "The less fortunate make do with the feathers of a wild turkey, or, better yet, of the first unlucky rooster that crosses their path. My poor fowls, all through the time of the payment, were plucked absolutely bare.", "When they are ready, the dancers gather at some convenient place and come marching to the appointed ground with the Indian drum and the shee-shee-qua, or rattle, going. They form a circle and dance with violent contortions and gestures, some of them graceful and some of them only strenuous. The squaws stand a little off to one side and put their own harsh voices in among the instruments, and they rarely dance themselves. Sometimes, though, when the general gaiety has got into them, a few will make a circle outside the men and do a kind of ungraceful up-and-down movement whose only merit is its perfect time — and the Indians, without a single exception I ever met, seem born with an ear for time. The dance ends only when the dancers have nothing left in them. Then a quantity of presents are brought and piled in the middle of the circle by order of the person who was complimented. One of their own number divides it all out fairly, and, the whole purpose of the display having been served, they go.", "The medicine dance is held mainly to celebrate the Medicine-man's skill in curing disease. He belongs to a fraternity credited with the further power of interpreting the will of the Great Spirit concerning the conduct of his people. He sometimes makes offerings and sacrifices that are understood to be propitiatory, and in that sense the word priest fits him. He is a prophet too, so far as he is in a limited way a teacher — but he makes no claim to foretell what is coming.", "A man is picked for the fraternity of the Medicine-man by those already in it, and generally because they have noticed some skill or shrewdness in him. Sometimes a man who has been dangerously ill and has recovered under the treatment of one of the members is judged a fit choice, on the strength of the claim that recovery establishes.", "When he is to be initiated, there is a great feast, paid for, naturally, by the candidate himself, because in plain life as in civilized life the same rule of politics holds: honors must be paid for. An animal is killed and dressed and the people at large eat of it, and there are dances and songs and speeches without end. Then the chief Medicine-man takes the candidate aside and instructs him privately in all the ceremonies and all the knowledge that make a member complete. Now and then the new member chosen is still a child. In that case the Medicine-man takes him as soon as he is old enough and brings him up by instruction and example into a creditable member of the fraternity.", "There is a good deal of belief in magic among the Winnebagoes. Every Medicine-man has a bag or sack that is understood to have some animal shut inside it, and in the course of his pow-wows he speaks to that animal, calling out in the cry proper to the species he supposes it to be. The people appear entirely satisfied that the answers he announces are real communications, in that form, from the Great Spirit.", "The Indians seem to have no notion of any reckoning beyond this life. They hold hard to the great fundamental virtues of natural religion — the worship of the Great Spirit, brotherly love, love of one's children, honesty, temperance, chastity. Any breach of the Great Spirit's laws, by a falling away from those virtues, they believe will rouse his anger and bring punishment down. Those are their principles. That their practice shows a wider and wider departure from them, under the debasing influence of living close to the whites, is a melancholy truth, and no one will admit it with so much sorrow as the people who lived among them, and thought well of them, before that change set in.", "One of the first improvements that occurred to us about the new house was getting rid of some very ugly pickets standing around two or three Indian graves on the esplanade out in front. Burial-places are held in such reverence, though, that we understood we would have to come at the subject with a great deal of delicacy and care.", "My husband did finally venture to propose to Mrs. Pawnee Blanc, the nearest living relative of the person buried there, that the pickets be replaced with a neat wooden platform.", "The idea pleased her very much, since through her closeness to Paquette's family she had picked up something of a taste for civilization. So a little platform about a foot high, properly finished off with a moulding around the edge, went in where the worn black pickets had been, and it was a touching thing to see the mournful satisfaction with which two or three old women came every evening at sunset, regular as the light itself, to sit and gossip over the ashes of their dead.", "On fine moonlit nights, too, you would often see a group of them sitting there in what is to them a solemn hour, for they hold the poetic belief that the moon was made to give light to the dead.", "The reverence the Indians pay to the memory of their departed friends, and the faithfulness with which they visit their graves and make offerings there to the Great Spirit, is an example their more enlightened brethren would do well to copy. Some of their other customs regarding the dead are another matter."],
  p3s22: ["A death in the village is the signal for a general mourning, and the mourning is loud. It is also — and I am sorry to have to set this down — sometimes the excuse for a general carouse, wherever the means and the appliances for one can be found.", "The relatives weep and howl for grief. The friends and acquaintances keep them company out of sympathy, and howl with them. Then a few of their number are appointed to go and wait upon their Father, to tell him what has happened, and to ask him for presents — to help them, as they put it, dry up their tears.", "We had such a visit one morning, not long after the payment was concluded.", "The dead man was a small drunken Indian whom the French people around had named \"Old Boilvin,\" after an Indian Agent of that name at Prairie du Chien, because they thought he resembled him. He had been fishing, they said, on the shore of one of the little lakes near the Portage. He had taken a little too much whiskee. He had gone into the water and had not come out again. Nothing of him had been found but his blanket, lying on the bank where he had left it. There could be no funeral ceremonies without a body. But his friends were fully prepared to make a great lamentation over him.", "Their Father weighed the case and gave them tobacco, knives, calico, and looking-glasses, in about the proportion he judged their grief could reasonably run at the loss of so worthless a vagabond. Then they went away.", "A keg of whiskey was got, of course. The prohibitions on that subject were stringent, and it made no difference at all; some of the traders were still lingering about the Portage, and they were obliging. Armed with the keg and with their other treasures, the mourners gathered at an appointed spot, not far from the scene of the catastrophe, and sat down in a ring with the keg in the middle of them, and began their affliction. The more they drank, the more clamorous the grief became. The tears came faster and faster.", "In the middle of these demonstrations a small figure came toward them out of the dusk, bent and staggering, covered over with mud, everything about him in disorder, his face full of wonder and sympathy. \"Why? what? what?\" he said. \"Who's dead?\"", "\"Who's dead?\" they repeated, looking up at him in astonishment. \"Why, you're dead! You were drowned in Swan Lake! Didn't we find your blanket there? Come, sit down and help us mourn.\"", "The old man did not wait to be asked a second time. He took his place among them and cried and drank with the rest, weeping and lamenting as bitterly as any of them; and the strange scene went on as long as they had power to articulate, or any portion of the whiskey was left."],
  p3s23: ["Whatever the tribe, they are hungry for stories — to tell them and to sit and hear them told, history or invention, it makes no difference which. They keep professed storytellers, as the nations of the East do, and the man goes from village to village and gathers a crowd around him that listens hard and admires everything, though there is not a word in the recitation that any of them is hearing for the first time.", "That is how the traditions come down whole, generation after generation, with nothing worn off them. Their knowledge of the geography of their own country is exact in a way that would shame a surveyor. I have watched a man sit in his lodge and draw the Northwestern States in the ashes with one finger — not the country as the statistics have it, but the shape of the land itself, the lakes, the rivers, the high ground — and get it right, and give the relative distances in days' journeys without once stopping to think, and then carry the drawing further south and keep talking, down as far as Kentucky and Tennessee.", "Their biography works the same way. They keep not only the leading events of a man's life but his features, his appearance, his bearing, his manners, and whatever small peculiarity it was that made him himself and nobody else.", "The women prefer fiction, and some of their stories mix humor and grief in a way I have met nowhere else. I set down two of them here as specimens. The Indian names in them are Ottawa — Courte-Oreilles — but the same tales are current in every tongue and dialect on this frontier.", "A great many peculiarities are attributed to this animal. They say he is like the jackal, that he will trouble the graves of the dead. They dread hearing his bark at night, because it forebodes calamity, and a death with it. And they say he was not always as he is now. He was one uniform color once, a plain reddish brown, all of him, legs and all. The legs went black in the way the story tells.", "There was a chief of a certain village who had a beautiful daughter. He decided one time to make a feast and to invite all the animals. When the invitation was carried to the red fox, the fox asked, \"What are you going to have for supper?\"", "\"Mee-dau-mee-nau-bo,\" the messenger said. That is a porridge made of parched corn, slightly cracked.", "The fox turned up his little sharp nose. \"No, I thank you,\" he said. \"I can get plenty of that at home.\"", "The messenger went back to the chief and reported the contemptuous refusal of the fox.", "\"Go back to him,\" said the chief, \"and tell him we are going to have a nice fresh body, and we will have it cooked in the most delicate manner possible.\"", "The prospect of such a treat pleased the fox very much, and he gave a hearty assent to the second invitation.", "The hour came and he set off for the lodge of the chief to attend the feast. The company were all prepared for him. They had made common cause with their friend who had been insulted. As the fox came in at the door, the guest nearest it rose with great courtesy and begged the newcomer to take his seat. Right away the next man rose too and insisted that the fox should have his place instead, as it was nearer the fire — the post of honor. Then the third pressed him, with many expressions of civility, to exchange with him. And so, with a great many ceremonious flourishes, he was passed along the circle, always drawing nearer to the fire, where a huge cauldron stood with the good cheer still cooking in it. The fox was by no means unwilling to occupy the highest place in the assembly. He was also anxious to get a look into the kettle, because he had begun to suspect that he might be disappointed of the delicacies he had been promised.", "So by degrees he was ushered nearer and nearer the great blazing fire, until with one dexterous push and shove he was hoisted into the seething kettle.", "His feet were dreadfully scalded. He leaped out of it and ran home to his own lodge, howling and crying with the pain. His grandmother, with whom he lived, according to the custom of animals, demanded an account of the whole affair. He told her every circumstance faithfully — unlike the civilized animals, it never occurred to him to tell his grandmother a story — and when he had finished she reproved him very strongly.", "\"You have committed two great faults,\" she said. \"In the first place you were very rude to the chief, who was kind enough to invite you, and by returning insult for civility you made yourself enemies who were determined to punish you. In the second place it was very unbecoming in you to be so forward to take the place of honor. Had you been content to keep your seat modestly near the door, you would have escaped the misfortune that has befallen you.\"", "None of this was very consolatory to the poor fox, who went on whining and crying most piteously while his grandmother, her lecture finished, went to bind up his wounds. It is thought that great virtue is added to any medicine or application by a little dancing. So the dressing was put on, and then the grandmother fell to dancing with all her might, round and round the lodge.", "When she was nearly exhausted the fox said, \"Grandmother, take off the bandages and see if my legs are healed.\"", "She did as he asked. The burns were still fresh. She danced and danced again. Now and then, as he grew impatient, she would take off the coverings to observe the effect of the remedies. Toward morning at last she looked, and sure enough, the burns were quite healed. \"But, oh!\" she cried, \"your legs are as black as a coal! They were so badly burned that they will never return to their color!\"", "The poor fox, who like many another brave was vain of his legs, fell into a transport of lamentation.", "\"Oh! my legs! My pretty red legs! What shall I do? The young girls will all despise me. I shall never dare to show myself among them again!\"", "He cried and sobbed until his grandmother, worn out with her exercise, fell asleep. By that time he had decided upon his plan of revenge.", "He got up and stole softly out of the lodge and made his way quickly toward the village of the chief. There he turned his face in the direction of the principal lodge, and barked. The people inside heard that sound come out of the stillness of the night, and their hearts trembled in them. They knew what it foreboded: sorrow and trouble to some one of their number.", "Very little time passed before the beautiful daughter of the chief fell sick. She grew worse and worse, in spite of the medicines and the charms and the dances. In the end she died. The fox had not intended to bring the misfortune down on the village in that particular shape, for he loved the beautiful daughter of the chief, and he kept close in his lodge and mourned and fretted over her death.", "They made preparations for a magnificent funeral, but the friends of the dead girl were in great perplexity. \"If we bury her in the earth,\" they said, \"the fox will come and disturb her remains. He has barked her to death, and he will be glad enough to come and finish his work of revenge.\"", "They took counsel together and determined to hang her body high in a tree for a place of sepulture. Their reasoning was that the fox would go groping about in the earth for her, and would never lift up his eyes to the branches over his head.", "But the grandmother had been at the funeral. She came home and told the fox everything that had been done.", "\"Now, my son,\" she said, \"listen to me. Don't meddle with the remains of the chief's daughter. You have done mischief enough already. Leave her in peace.\"", "As soon as the grandmother was asleep that night, the fox went out. He found the place he was looking for without much trouble, and he came and sat down under the tree where the young girl had been put. He gazed and gazed at her all the livelong night, and she looked as beautiful as she had in life. But when the day came on, and the light let him see more clearly, he saw that decay was doing its work — that what hung above him was not beautiful at all now, but loathsome.", "He went home sad and afflicted and passed the whole day mourning in his lodge.", "\"Have you disturbed the remains of the chief's beautiful daughter?\" was his parent's anxious question.", "\"No, grandmother.\" And he uttered not another word.", "So it went on for many days and many nights. The fox always took care to leave his watch at the first dawn of day, because he knew that her friends would suspect him, and would come early to see whether all was as they had left it.", "And in time he began to notice that she looked less and less hideous in the morning light — that by degrees she was resuming the appearance she had worn in life, until at last her beauty and her look of health had quite come back to her.", "One day he said, \"Grandmother, give me my pipe, that I may take a smoke.\"", "\"Ah!\" she cried, \"you begin to be comforted. You have never smoked since the death of the chief's beautiful daughter. Have you heard some good news?\"", "\"Never you mind,\" he said. \"Bring the pipe.\"", "He sat down and smoked, and smoked. After a while he said, \"Grandmother, sweep your lodge and put it all in order, for this day you will receive a visit from your daughter-in-law.\"", "The grandmother did as she was told. She swept the lodge and arranged it with all the taste she possessed, and then the two of them sat down to wait for the visit.", "\"When you hear a sound at the door,\" the fox said, \"you must give the salutation, and say, Come in.\"", "They had been sitting that way for some time when the grandmother heard a faint rustling sound. She looked toward the door. The mat that usually hung there as a curtain had been rolled up, and the door stood open.", "\"Peen-tee-geen n'dau-nis!\" she cried. Come in, my daughter.", "Something like a faint, faint shadow seemed to glide in. It took on a more distinct outline by degrees. As she looked and looked at it she began to make out the form and features of the chief's beautiful daughter; but it was a long while before she appeared like a reality, and took her place in the lodge like a thing of flesh and blood.", "They kept the matter hidden very close, for they would not for the world have had the father or the friends of the bride learn what had happened. Before long, though, it began to be rumored about that the chief's beautiful daughter had returned to life and was living in the Red Fox's lodge. How it ever became known was a mystery, for the grandmother, of course, never spoke of it.", "However it got out, the news made a great excitement in the village. \"This must never be,\" they all said. \"He barked her to death once, and who knows what he may do the next time?\"", "The father took a decided part at once. \"The Red Fox isn't worthy of my daughter,\" he said. \"I had promised her to the Hart, the finest and most elegant among the animals. Now that she has returned to life, I shall keep my word.\"", "So the friends all went in a body to the lodge of the Red Fox. The bridegroom and the bride and the grandmother made all the resistance they could, but they were overpowered by numbers, and the Hart, who had waited conveniently outside where there was no danger, had the beautiful daughter of the chief put up on his back, and he coursed away through the forest to carry her home. When he reached the door of his own lodge he turned his head, and there was no bride in the place where he expected to see her. He had thought his burden very light from the beginning, but that had seemed to him natural enough in a spirit returned from the dead. It never entered his mind that she had glided from her seat at the very outset, and had slipped back in the middle of the tumult, unobserved, to her chosen husband.", "One or two more attempts were made after that by her friends to repossess themselves of the young creature, and all of them failed. Then they said, \"Let her stay where she is. It is true the Red Fox occasioned her death, but by his watchfulness and his care he caressed her into life again, so she rightfully belongs to him.\" And the Red Fox and his beautiful bride lived long together in great peace and happiness."],
  p3s24: ["There was a young man named Shee-shee-banze, the Little Duck, and he was paddling his canoe along the shore of the lake.", "Two girls came down to the edge of the water, and when they saw him the elder said to the younger, \"Let us call to him to take us a sail.\"", "It has to be said that in all Indian stories where two or more sisters are the persons of the drama, the elder is invariably represented as silly, ridiculous, and disgusting, and the younger as wise and beautiful.", "In this case the younger remonstrated. \"Oh, no,\" she said. \"Let us not do such a thing. What will he think of us?\"", "But the other persevered and called out to him, \"Ho! come and take us into your canoe.\" The young man obeyed. He brought the canoe in to the shore and took them aboard with him.", "\"Who are you?\" asked the elder sister.", "\"I am Way-gee-mar-kin,\" he replied. \"The great chief.\"", "Now this Way-gee-mar-kin was something of a fairy. When he was surrounded by his followers and had a mind to confer favors on them, he had a habit of coughing slightly, and out of his mouth would fly quantities of silver brooches and ear-bobs and other ornaments, for which it was the custom of his people to scramble, each of them striving, as in more civilized life, to come away with more than his share.", "So the elder sister said, \"If you are Way-gee-mar-kin, let us see you cough.\"", "Shee-shee-banze had a few of these silver ornaments, which he had got by scrambling for them, and which he kept stowed away in the sides of his mouth in case of emergency. He gave two or three spasmodic coughs and brought forth a few of them, and the girl seized on them eagerly.", "They paddled on, and after a time a fine noble elk came out of the forest and down to the water to drink.", "\"What is that?\" asked the spokeswoman, for the younger sister sat silent and modest the whole time.", "\"It is my dog, that I hunt with.\"", "\"Call him to us, that I may see him.\"", "Shee-shee-banze called. The elk turned and fled into the woods.", "\"He doesn't seem to obey you, though.\"", "\"No. It is because you inspire him with disgust, and so he flies from you.\"", "Soon a bear appeared at the water's edge.", "\"What is that?\"", "\"One of my servants.\"", "Again he was asked to call the creature, and again the call was disregarded, and the same reason as before was assigned for it.", "Their excursion came to an end at last. There had been a little magic in it. Although the young girls had believed themselves to be sitting in a canoe, there was in reality no canoe at all. They had only imagined it to be so.", "Now, Shee-shee-banze lived with his grandmother, and it was to her lodge that he conducted his young friends.", "They stood outside while he went in.", "\"Grandmother,\" he said, \"I have brought you two young girls, who will be your daughters-in-law. Invite them into your lodge.\"", "At that the old woman called out, \"Ho! come in,\" and they entered. They were made welcome and treated to the best of everything.", "In the meantime the real Way-gee-mar-kin, the great chief, was making preparations for a grand feast. When he was sending his messenger out with the invitations he said to him, \"Be very particular to bid Shee-shee-banze to the feast, for, as he is the smallest and meanest person in the tribe, you must use double ceremony with him, or he will be apt to think himself slighted.\"", "Shee-shee-banze was sitting in his lodge with his new friends when the messenger arrived.", "\"Ho! Shee-shee-banze,\" he cried, \"you are invited to a great feast that Way-gee-mar-kin is to give tonight, to all his subjects.\"", "Shee-shee-banze took no notice of the invitation whatever. He only whistled and pretended not to hear. The messenger said the words over again, and then, finding that no attention was being paid to them, he went his way.", "The young girls looked at each other during this scene, greatly astonished. At last the elder spoke.", "\"What does this mean?\" she said. \"Why does he call you Shee-shee-banze, and invite you to visit Way-gee-mar-kin?\"", "\"Oh,\" said Shee-shee-banze, \"it is one of my followers that always likes to be a little impudent. I am obliged to put up with it sometimes, but you noticed that I treated him with silent contempt.\"", "The messenger went back to the chief and reported the manner in which the invitation had been received.", "\"Oh,\" said the good-natured chief, \"it is because he feels that he is poor and insignificant. Go back again — call him by my name, and make a flourishing speech to him.\"", "The messenger fulfilled his mission exactly as he had been bid.", "\"Way-gee-mar-kin,\" he said, pompously, \"a great feast is to be given tonight, and I am sent most respectfully to solicit the honor of your company!\"", "\"Did I not tell you?\" said Shee-shee-banze to the maidens. Then, nodding with careless condescension, he added, \"Tell them I'll come.\"", "That night he dressed himself in his very best paint and feathers and ornaments; but before he set out, he took his grandmother aside.", "\"Be sure,\" he said, \"that you watch these young people closely until I come back. Shut up your lodge tight, tight. Let no one come in or go out, and, above all things, don't go to sleep.\"", "Those orders given, he went his way.", "The grandmother tried her best to stay awake. As the night wore on and she felt herself getting sleepier and sleepier, she took a strong cord and laced it back and forth across the mat that hung before the entrance of the lodge, the way they lace up the mouths of their bags. Then, having seen everything secure and the girls quiet in their bed, she lay down and soon fell into a comfortable sleep.", "The young girls, in the meanwhile, were dying of curiosity to know what had become of Shee-shee-banze, and as soon as they were certain the old lady was asleep they made ready to follow him and see what was going on. They were afraid, though, that the grandmother might wake and find them gone. So they took two logs of wood and put them under the blanket and arranged them to present the appearance of people sleeping quietly. Then they cut the cords that fastened the door, and, guided by the sounds of the music and the dancing and the merry-making, they soon found their way to the house of Way-gee-mar-kin.", "When they came in they saw the chief seated on a throne, surrounded by light and splendor. Everything was joy and amusement. Crowds of courtiers filled the apartment, all of them dressed in the most brilliant array. The strangers looked around for their friend Shee-shee-banze. He was nowhere to be seen.", "Now and then the chief would cough, and a shower of silver ornaments and precious things would fly out in all directions, and instantly a scramble would begin among the company to gather them up and appropriate them.", "As they rushed forward that way, the brides-elect saw their poor little friend crowded up into a corner, where nobody took any notice of him except to push him aside, or to step on him whenever he was in the way. He gave out piteous little squeaks as one and another of them maltreated him. He was too busy taking care of himself to perceive that the two he had left snug at home in the lodge were witnesses of the whole of it.", "At last the signal was given for the company to retire — all but the two young damsels, on whom Way-gee-mar-kin had set his eye, and to whom he had sent, by one of his assistants, great offers to induce them to stay with him and become his wives.", "Poor Shee-shee-banze came home to his lodge, and what was his consternation to find the door standing open.", "\"Ho! grandmother,\" he cried, \"is this the way you keep watch?\"", "The old woman started up. \"There are my daughters-in-law,\" she said, and pointed to the two logs of wood. Shee-shee-banze threw himself down on the ground between them. His back was broken by coming so violently in contact with them, but he did not mind that. He was thinking only of revenge, and of getting his sweethearts back.", "He waited only long enough to get a powerful poison and prepare it, and then he stole back to the wigwam of Way-gee-mar-kin. Everything was silent. He crept in without making the slightest noise. There lay the chief, with a young girl on either side of him.", "All three of them were sound asleep. The chief was lying on his back with his mouth wide open. Before he knew anything about it the poison was down his throat, and Shee-shee-banze had retreated quietly to his own lodge.", "The next morning the cry went through the village that Way-gee-mar-kin had been found dead in his bed. It was attributed, of course, to over-indulgence at the feast. Everything was grief and lamentation. \"Let us go and tell poor Shee-shee-banze,\" said one of them. \"He was so fond of Way-gee-mar-kin.\"", "They found him sitting on a bank, fishing. He had been up since the peep of day, making his preparations for receiving the intelligence.", "He had caught two or three fish, and had taken out their bladders and filled them with blood and tied them under his arm. When the friends of Way-gee-mar-kin caught sight of him they called out, \"Oh! Shee-shee-banze — your friend, Way-gee-mar-kin, is dead!\"", "With a gesture of despair Shee-shee-banze drew his knife and plunged it — not into his heart, but into the bladders full of blood that he had prepared. As he fell to the ground, apparently lifeless, the messengers began to reproach themselves. \"Oh! why did we tell him so suddenly? We might have known he wouldn't survive it. Poor Shee-shee-banze! he loved Way-gee-mar-kin so.\"", "To their great surprise, the day after the funeral, Shee-shee-banze came walking toward the wigwam of the dead chief. As he walked he sang, or rather chaunted, to a monotonous strain, the following: \"Way-gee-mar-kin is dead, is dead, I know who killed him.", "I guess it was I — I guess it was I.\"", "The whole village was roused. Everybody flew in pursuit of the murderer, but he evaded them and escaped to a place of safety.", "Soon after that he appeared again, mincing as he walked, and singing to the same strain as before: \"If you wish to take and punish me, Let the widows come and catch me.\"", "That seemed a good idea, and the young women were recommended to go and entice the culprit into the village, so that the friends of the deceased could lay hold of him.", "They went forth on their errand. Shee-shee-banze would suffer them to come close, and then he would dance off a little way; now he would let them come quite near him, and then he would retreat a little before them, singing the whole time, \"Come, pretty widows, come and catch me.\"", "So he decoyed them on, using honeyed words and flattering speeches now and then, until he had gained their consent to come back with him to his lodge and take up their abode with him.", "The friends of the murdered chief were scandalized at such inconstancy, and resolved to punish all three of them as soon as they could be caught.", "They surrounded his lodge with cries and threatenings, but Shee-shee-banze and his two brides had managed to elude their vigilance and get to his canoe, which lay in the river close at hand.", "They were hardly on board when the escape was discovered. The whole troop flew after them. Some plunged into the stream and seized hold of the canoe. In the struggle it was upset; but the moment they touched the water — whether from the magical properties of the canoe, or from the necromantic skill of the grandmother — they were all transformed into ducks, and flew quacking away.", "Since that time the water-fowl of this species are always found in companies of three: two females and a male.", "The Canard de France, or Mallard, and the Brancheuse, or Wood Duck, have different habits from these, and fly in pairs. Indeed the constancy of the latter is said to be so great that if he loses his mate he never takes another partner, but goes mourning to the end of his days."],
  p3s25: ["The payment was over and the Indians had dispersed, and we set about settling down quietly in our little home. Then a new source of disturbance arose.", "My husband's accounts of disbursements as Agent of the Winnebagoes, which he had forwarded to the Department at Washington, had never arrived there. He received notice of the fact in due course — that is to say, such notice as could reach us at all, by the circuitous and uncertain mode of conveyance which was then the only communication we had with the Eastern world. If the vouchers for the former expenditures could not be produced, along with those for the recent payment of fifteen thousand dollars of annuity money, it might place him in a very awkward position indeed. He decided to go at once to Washington and carry the duplicate accounts himself.", "\"Should you like to go and see your father and mother,\" he said to me one morning, \"and show them how the West agrees with you?\"", "After a year's separation it was the most joyful suggestion in the world, and in a few days everything was in readiness for our departure.", "We had a visitor at the time — Miss Brush, of Detroit, who had come up from Green Bay with Mr. and Mrs. Whitney and Miss Frances Henshaw on an excursion to the Mississippi. Our little India-rubber house had managed to stretch itself to accommodate the whole party for the length of their very pleasant visit.", "The arrival of two young ladies had been, as anyone may imagine, a godsend to the unmarried lieutenants; and when Miss Brush — tired of the trip, or else intimidated by the snow, which fell eight inches deep on the fourth of October — determined to give up the rest of her excursion and accept our pressing invitation to stay with us until her friends came back, we were looked upon as public benefactors. She was now to accompany us to Green Bay, and possibly on to Detroit.", "Our voyage down the river passed without incident, and we reached Green Bay just as the whole place was astir over the expected arrival of one of Mr. Newbery's schooners. That was an event, and the interest in it ran from Fort Howard the length of the settlement down to Dickenson's. To some the schooner would bring friends and to some supplies; to the ladies she brought the fashions and to the gentlemen the news, for she was the bearer of the mails — not for that place alone, but for the whole of the upper country.", "In a few days the vessel came in. She had a mail aboard for Fort Winnebago, letters being carried overland to that place, by way of Niles's Settlement and Chicago, only in the winter season.", "By virtue of his office as Postmaster my husband opened the mail-bag and took possession of his own letters. One of them told him that the missing accounts had turned up at the Department, and that all was satisfactory there. The next one I remember as a plain drop of the heart. My parents had gone to Kentucky for the winter. Not to any city, nor to any place a person could reach, but up the Sandy, and over among the mountains of Virginia, hunting up old land-claims belonging to my grandfather's estate.", "It was vain to hope to follow them. We could hardly expect to come up with them in the short time we could be absent from home — not even if we had at our disposal the lucid directions once given to my father by an old settler, during his own explorations through that wild region.", "\"You must go up Tug,\" the man had told him, \"and down Troublesome, and fall over on to Kingdom-come.\" We did not think it advisable to undertake an expedition of that description, and so we made up our minds to retrace our steps to Fort Winnebago."],
  p3s26: ["There were no boats ready to ascend the river. Our old friend Hamilton promised to have one in preparation at once, and time passed, and no boat was made ready.", "It was the beginning of November now. We were passing our days pleasantly enough with the Irwins and the Whitneys, and at the residence of Colonel Stambaugh, the Indian Agent, but the delay was inconvenient and it was becoming vexatious.", "I suggested making the trip on horseback. \"No, indeed,\" was the answer I got, every time I raised it. \"No mortal woman has ever gone that road, unless it was some native on foot, nor ever could.\"", "\"But suppose we set out in the boat and get frozen in on the way. We can neither pass the winter there, nor possibly find our way to a human home. We have had one similar experience already. Is it not better to take it for granted that I can do what you and others of your sex have done?\"", "Dr. Finley, the post-surgeon at Fort Howard, heard the matter being debated and offered me his favorite horse Charlie on the spot. He was very sure-footed, the doctor said, and capital in a marsh or a troublesome stream.", "By land, then, it was decided we should go; and as soon as our old Menomonee friend Wish-tay-yun, who was as good a guide over ground as over water, could be summoned, we set off — leaving our trunks behind to be forwarded by Hamilton whenever it should please him to carry out his intention of sending up his boat.", "On the morning of our departure we waited until a late hour for our fellow-travelers, Mr. Wing, of Monroe, and Dr. Philleo, of Galena. They did not come. We resolved to lose no more time, being confident that we should all meet at the Kakalin in the course of the evening."],
  p3s26a: ["We crossed the river at the place that is now Depere and rode into the wild, unsettled country on the west bank, and there we found a succession of wooded hills divided from one another by ravines so narrow and so steep that it seemed impossible any animal but a mule or a goat could make its way among them.", "Wish-tay-yun took the lead. The horse he rode knew the country and was well trained to that style of road. As for Charlie, he was perfectly admirable. When he came to a precipitous descent he would set his forefeet out ahead of him and slide down on his haunches in the most scientific manner, while my only mode of preserving my balance was to hold fast by the bridle and lay myself braced almost flat along his back. Then our position would change all at once and we would be scaling the opposite bank, at the imminent risk of falling backward into the ravine below.", "It was amusing to watch Wish-tay-yun as he scrambled on ahead, turning half round now and then to see how I was faring. And when at last I came up over the summit, panting and laughing, he would throw up both hands and shout, with the utmost glee, \"Mamma Manitou!\" My mother is a spirit.", "Our old acquaintances the Grignons seemed much surprised that I should have ventured on such a trip. They had never undertaken it themselves, although they had lived so long at the Kakalin; but then there was no reason why they should have. They could always command a canoe or a boat whenever they wished to visit the Bay.", "As we had anticipated, our gentlemen joined us at supper. They had delayed to take dinner with Colonel Stambaugh. They had had a delightful gallop up from the Bay. They had seen no ravines, nor anything but fine smooth roads. They might have been asleep, but if so they were not conscious of it. That was the account they gave of themselves, to our no small amusement."],
  p3s26b: ["Our next day's stage was from the Kakalin to the Butte des Morts, where a man named Knaggs lived. The country was rough and wild, much like the ground we had passed through the spring before in going from Hamilton's diggings to Kellogg's Grove; but we had the good fortune to have Wish-tay-yun for our guide instead of Uncle Billy, so that we were able to make our way with some degree of moderation.", "We had traveled only forty miles when we reached Knaggs's, and yet I was both cold and worn out, so that the cosy little room where we found Mrs. Knaggs, and the bright fire in it, were the most cheering objects in the world. We had broken our fast since morning with nothing but a few crackers carried in our pockets, and I must own that we did ample justice to her coffee and her cakes, and to the venison-steaks and the bear's meat besides — the last of which I had never tasted before.", "Supper over, we looked about us for a place of repose. The room we had eaten in was small, just large enough to hold a bed, a table set against the wall, and the few chairs we had been sitting on. There was no room for a shakedown of any kind.", "\"Where can you put us for the night?\" my husband asked Mr. Knaggs, when he made his appearance.", "\"Why, there is no place that I know of, unless you can camp down in the old building outside.\"", "We went out to look at it. It was one room, bare and dirty. A huge chimney with a few brands burning in it took up nearly one whole side of the place. Against another wall a rickety sort of bunk had been built. That was the only vestige of furniture to be seen anywhere. The floor lay thick with mud and dirt, and in the middle of it, near the fire, an old Indian was sitting with a pan of boiled corn in his lap, scooping it up with both hands and devouring it with the utmost voracity.", "We soon discovered that he was blind. At the sound of footsteps and voices he gathered the dish of food in close against himself, and began some morose grumbling. But when he was told that it was Shaw-nee-aw-kee who was speaking to him, his features relaxed into a more agreeable expression, and he even held the dish out and invited us to share what was in it.", "\"But are we to stay here?\" I asked. \"Can we not sleep out-of-doors?\"", "\"We have no tent,\" my husband replied, \"and the weather is too cold to risk the exposure without one.\"", "\"I could sit in a chair all night, by the fire.\"", "\"Then you wouldn't be able to ride to Bellefontaine tomorrow.\"", "There was no alternative. The only thing Mr. Knaggs could furnish in the shape of bedding was a small bear-skin. The bunk was a trifle less filthy than the floor, so on its boards we spread first the skin and then our saddle-blankets, and with a pair of saddle-bags for a bolster I wrapped myself in my cloak and resigned myself to my distasteful accommodations.", "Simply changing position from the one I had held all day probably brought me some rest, but sleep I could not. Even on a softer and pleasanter couch there would have been no sleeping through the snoring of the old Indian and the two or three companions who had joined him, or through his frequent querulous exclamations whenever he felt himself encroached upon in the darkness.", "It was a relief to get up with the early morning and prepare for the day's travel. Where our fellow-travelers had bestowed themselves I never learned, but they had evidently fared no better than we had. They were in fine spirits nonetheless. We took our breakfast cheerfully and were ferried over the river, to go on from that point along the trail to Bellefontaine, twelve miles distant from Fort Winnebago."],
  p3s26c: ["Everything anyone had told us about this road came down in the end to one name, and at about nine o'clock we came to it. Ma-zhee-gaw-gaw Swamp lay across our way, half a mile from edge to edge and running off to either side further than the eye could follow. Water stood over much of it. Out of the water rose black knobs, scattered anyhow, and those knobs were all the footing our animals were going to get. Where there was no water there was mud instead, black and slick and faintly moving, the sort of surface that looks as though it will open under a hoof and let the leg go down as far as it likes.", "That was what we had to cross. All three of the gentlemen pushed out ahead of me, each one announcing that he meant to find the safest and firmest line for me to follow. One would call back, \"Here, madam, come this way!\" Another would call, \"This is the best path, wifie, follow me.\" Charlie, more often than not, was of a different opinion, and chose his own route, and his was the best route in the swamp.", "He took it slowly. He would stand on one little rise of ground and study the next, and step across, and stand again, and now and then he would refuse a stretch of mud altogether and go around it, and by this method the rest of the party gradually drew away from us. I had been watching nothing but my horse's feet. When at last I lifted my eyes, this is what I saw: my husband down off his horse, hauling at the bridle to get the animal out of the place it had walked itself into; Mr. Wing's great white beast lying over on its side in the mud, floundering, with Mr. Wing working to free himself from the stirrups; and Dr. Philleo standing a little apart from his own steed, which had pitched into a deep bog and was heaving to get up out of it. It was a formidable sight. All three of them found their voices at once. \"Oh, don't come this way!\"", "\"Indeed,\" cried I, \"I have no thought of it. Charlie and I know better.\" I left it entirely to the sagacious creature, and he went on picking and choosing until he had carried me safely past the whole dismounted company. I looked back at them climbing toward their saddles again, and I am afraid I could not keep myself from a small triumphant flourish of the whip.", "Three hours we spent in that place, and still we were not out of the Slough of Despond. At last the far edge came in sight — and with it a stream, deep, and five or six feet across. The gentlemen reached it first, got down, and stood there debating.", "\"Jump off, jump off, madam,\" cried Mr. Wing, and \"Jump off, jump off,\" echoed Dr. Philleo, \"we are just consulting how we are to get you across.\"", "\"What do you think about it?\" asked my husband.", "\"Charlie will show you,\" replied I. \"Come, Charlie.\" I lifted the bridle smartly, gave his neck a pat and made the little chirping sound he knew, and he went over that water as lightly as a deer and set me down on solid ground.", "Of the whole company Mr. Wing had come off worst. The clumsy animal he was riding held the view that whenever he found himself in difficulty his part was to lie down and submit to whatever came next, and his rider, who was neither light nor especially quick, was generally beneath him and half bedded in the mire before he had settled on what to do about it.", "So it was good sense in him, when he came to the little stream, to walk straight into it and wade across, and rinse off what he could of the morning from his horse and from himself. The other gentlemen had no better idea, and did the same."],
  p3s26d: ["We did not stay long on the rising ground beyond the morass. Forty-five miles lay between us and Bellefontaine, and not one of them was a short mile.", "The horses were magnificent all that afternoon. Charlie held a canter the whole way and never once broke it. But the light went out of the sky by degrees, and the landmarks that were to tell us we were near the desired haven did not come, and did not come.", "\"Can we not stop and rest for a few moments under one of the trees?\" I asked. I was nearly past holding myself upright, and a cold November rain had begun to come down on us and showed no sign of stopping.", "\"If it were possible, we would,\" he said. \"But see how dark it is growing. If we should lose our way, it would be worse than being wet and tired.\"", "So we kept on. Just at dark the horses splashed through a clear stream, and my husband said, \"That is, I think, two miles from Bellefontaine. Cheer up — we shall soon be there.\" Two miles. I could ride two miles. We went on more cheerfully, and the trees went by, and no light came friendly through them. Another mile. Another.", "\"We have certainly traveled more than six miles now,\" I said.", "\"Yes — that couldn't have been the two-mile creek.\"", "It was eight o'clock when we reached Bellefontaine. They brought us into a large room with a huge fire blazing in it, and the heat of it went through me like something poured. Mr. Wing and Dr. Philleo had come in ahead of us, and there were other travelers besides, on their way up from the Mississippi. The hostess was an immense woman — la grosse Américaine, they all called her — and she received me with a kindness and a torrent of talk that allowed no answer, installed me in the arm-chair in the warmest corner, and in due time set an excellent supper in front of us.", "Her hospitality stopped short of her bed, which was the only one in the house and stayed hers. Instead she spread every article she could muster on the hard floor before the fire and did what she could to make me comfortable on it. Then she watched my husband fretting over me, anxious the rain and the ride would make me ill, and she said, in a tone of genuine admiring sympathy, \"How kind your companion is to you!\" The expression was new to us then. We were tired enough that it struck us both as very funny.", "Our traveling companions set out early in the morning for the Fort, which was only twelve miles off, and were good enough to carry a note to our friends at home, asking them to send Plante with the carriage to bring us the rest of the way.", "We reached the Portage in safety. So ended the first trip by land that any white woman had made from Green Bay to Fort Winnebago. I confess I thought rather better of myself when my husband told me that the distance I had ridden the day before, from Knaggs's to Bellefontaine, was sixty-two miles."],
  p3s27: ["A few weeks after we got home, my husband took his mother down to Prairie du Chien to be seen by Dr. Beaumont, of the U.S. Army. She had needed the advice for some time, and there was no other way to get it.", "They went in a large open boat down the Wisconsin, and since the boat had to make the trip anyway it was decided to bring back a good supply of corn, enough to carry the men and the cattle through the winter.", "The ice caught them. It formed in the river so early that he had barely started up with his load before he had to turn and take the whole of it back to the Prairie, and sit there, and wait — wait until the winter's ice lay thick enough to hold sleighs, and then make the journey a second time and bring the corn up over the top of the river he could not travel on. That is what it cost, in time and labor and exposure, to move the plain necessities of life from one point to another in that wild and desolate country.", "My brother Arthur came up from Kentucky by way of the Mississippi in the latter part of April, and he brought the news with him. There was new trouble with the Sauks and Foxes. Black Hawk had recrossed the Mississippi with the flower of his nation, to take back the old homes and the old corn-fields. Our own Indians began coming in almost at once to confirm it and to tell us, over and over, that they meant to stay faithful friends to the Americans. Then came word that the Illinois Rangers were in the Rock River country, and that the regular force under General Atkinson was in pursuit of the hostile Indians — who, by every report that reached us, were never where the pursuit was. It is not their custom to stand and give battle. The Sauks simply scattered themselves through the country and waited on a lucky accident to let them fall upon their enemies unexpectedly. Such accidents came, alas, only too often.", "So the army's experience, for the most part, was this: a long, toilsome, punishing march to the place where the Sauks were certainly waiting to receive them, and then the discovery that the rogues had scampered off to some entirely different part of the country.", "Wherever they did go, they left a mark. The worst of it had not reached our ears yet. All we were told, at the time, was that they were down in the neighborhood of the Rock River and the Kishwaukee, and that they let no opportunity pass of falling upon defenceless inhabitants and murdering them cruelly.", "Once it was clear the Sauks and Foxes would not do what they had done the year before — retreat peaceably across the Mississippi — Mr. Kinzie resolved to hold a council with every principal chief of the Winnebagoes he could reach. He knew the Sauks would spend every effort trying to bring their neighbors in with them, and he knew, too, that in the breasts of too many of the young men there was a real hunger to distinguish themselves by taking some white scalps. They did not love the Americans. Why should they? By the Americans they had been dispossessed, piece by piece, of the broad and beautiful country of their forefathers, and hunted from place to place, and all they had received in exchange was a few thousands a year in silver and presents, together with the pernicious example, the debasing influence, and the outright ill treatment of far too many of the new settlers on their land."],
  p3s28: ["With all of that in front of him, their Father judged that nothing short of the utmost watchfulness would do, and that the strongest arguments he had would have to be spent keeping the young men of the Winnebagoes in their allegiance to the Americans. The older men he was sure of. About fifty lodges had come in when the trouble first started and made their camp around our house, and what they told us was that if the Sauks meant to get at us they would have to kill them first. We knew those men. We believed them absolutely.", "But having them close, while it made us feel guarded, also opened a channel straight into our parlor for every piece of news the country produced, and the news came daily, and none of it was good. As the fighting worked nearer and nearer, their runners brought it in raw. That Captain Barney's head had been recognized in the Sauk camp, where it had been carried the day before. Then that the Sauks were marching to meet the whites with Lieutenant Beall's head carried on a pole ahead of them. Sometimes a story turned out, unhappily, to be exactly true, as with the murder of their own Agent, M. St Vrain, at Kellogg's Grove — killed by the Sauks themselves, who of all people ought to have protected him."],
  p3s29: ["It was after that last piece of news that the appointed council with the Winnebagoes came due, to be held at the Four Lakes, thirty-five miles from Fort Winnebago.", "We pleaded with him. We argued. None of it moved him an inch. \"It was his duty to assemble his people and talk to them,\" my husband said, \"and he must run the risk, if there were any. He had perfect confidence in the Winnebagoes. The enemy, by all he could learn, were now far distant from the Four Lakes — probably at Kosh-ko-nong. He would set off early in the morning with Paquette, hold his council, and return to us the same evening.\"", "I will not try to describe that day. It was long and it was gray and there was nothing in it to do but wait through it. By nightfall we had come to the state where a drunken Indian calling out somewhere in the dark, or even a dog barking, went through us like a blade.", "We sat up late at the open window, listening to everything. And at last, out of all that listening, came the sound of horses. We knew the tread of Griffin and Jerry coming up the hill, and then a cheerful shout came up ahead of them, which meant that all was well. He and his interpreter had ridden seventy miles that day, and held a long talk with the Indians in the middle of it.", "The Winnebagoes in council had promised to do everything in their power to keep peace and good order among their young men. They told their Father that the bands on the Rock River, Win-no-sheek's excepted, were all determined to stay friendly and keep clear of the Sauks — and that to prove it they were giving up their villages and their corn-fields and moving north, so that their Great Father the President should have no cause to be dissatisfied with them. About Win-no-sheek and his people they said they could not answer.", "Time went on and brought more of the same. The murders of Auberry, Green, and Force at Blue Mound. The attack on Apple Fort. That last came to us from old Crély, the father of Mrs. Paquette, who rode express from Galena, and who swore he had passed within arm's reach of a bush with Sauks lying behind it — but his horse caught the smell of the sweet-scented grass they always wear on a war-party, and set off at a gallop that did not stop until it reached the Portage.", "Another man who brought us news was a young gentleman named Follett. He had spent so many days watching for the enemy that his eyes had come out and set, and it was a long while after he reached safety before they went back into their usual place and expression."],
  p3s29a: ["Among the rumors that came to us in those days was one that the Sauks were considering an attack on Fort Winnebago itself. That the fort was in no state to be attacked, the Indians knew perfectly well. Every effective man had been pulled out of it on a requisition from General Atkinson and sent to join him at his newly built fort at Kosh-ko-nong.", "Fort Winnebago was not picketed in. The barracks and the officers' quarters had no defences at all beyond thin panelled doors and Venetian blinds — nothing that would stand more than a minute against clubs or hatchets. There was no artillery. The Commissary's store was outside the bounds of the fort altogether, down under the hill.", "Mr. Kinzie had been pointing all of this out to the officers from the beginning. What he generally got back was a laugh.", "\"Never fear,\" they would say. \"The Sauks aren't coming here to attack us.\"", "One afternoon we were over in the garrison visiting some of the ladies, with several officers in the room, and the talk went where it always went now.", "\"Do you not think it wiser,\" I asked a blustering young officer, \"to be prepared against possible danger?\"", "\"Not against these fellows,\" he said, with contempt.", "\"I don't think I would even take the trouble to fasten the blinds to my quarters.\"", "\"At least,\" I said, \"if you some night find a tomahawk raised to cleave your skull, you will have the consolation of remembering that you haven't been one of those foolish fellows who keep on the safe side.\"", "That nettled him a little, and he was more nettled still when sister Margaret said: \"Personally, I am of Governor Cass's opinion. He was at Chicago during the Winnebago war. We were all preparing to move into the fort on the first alarm. Some were for being brave and delaying, like our friends here. 'Come, come,' said the Governor, 'hurry into the fort as fast as possible — there is no merit in being brave with the Indians. It is the height of folly to stay and meet danger which you may by prudence avoid.'\""],
  p3s29b: ["Within a few days our friends across the river woke up to the fact that something had to be done, and done at once. The first step was an order forbidding any Winnebago to come inside the garrison, in case they should find out what they had known just as well as we had for the past three months — how little there was in that place to resist anything. The next was to send fatigue-parties out into the woods under guard, to cut pickets to fence the garrison in.", "There was every reason to think the enemy were not far off. The best guess about their purpose in coming north was that they meant to break a road through into the Chippewa country, where they would be safe among friends and allies. But the story our Indian runners brought in most often was a different one. It was that the Sauks had determined to come down on the whites at the Portage and the Fort and massacre every one of them, sparing only the families of the Agent and the Interpreter.", "Plante and Pillon had taken their families out at the first word of danger. What was left to us was Manaigre, whose wife was half Winnebago, Isidore Morrin, and the two blacksmiths from Sugar Creek, Mâtâ and Turcotte.", "At night we were armed, all of us, and each had a post. Every possible thing was done to make the house secure, and then the orders were given. If we were attacked, sister Margaret and I were to take the children up to the rooms above while my husband and his men held the ground floor as long as they could hold it. I had lately shown my sportsmanship by bringing down a blackbird on the wing, entirely by accident, and on the strength of that I believed I could do some real execution with my little pistols. They were laid beside my pillow every night. I was fully resolved to use them. What I remember, and what I find I cannot explain away, is that the idea of taking the lives of two Sauks — which is exactly what I should have done — did not trouble me in the slightest. It taught me a thing I had wondered about all my life: how a soldier on a field of battle can be indifferent to the destruction of human life. Had I ever actually been called on to use those weapons to any effect, I have no doubt at all that I would have looked back on it with horror.", "We were ringed by Indian lodges, and a camp is never quite silent, and our nerves were in the state that a steady diet of such news puts them in. We slept badly. One night, long after we had settled ourselves as well as we could, there came a tap on the window at the head of our bed, and a voice calling: \"Chon! Chon!\" — John! John!"],
  p3s29c: ["\"Tshah-ko-zhah?\" — What is it?", "It was Hoo-wau-ne-kah, the Little Elk. He spoke fast, and he spoke in a tone I had never heard him use. I could not follow him. I lay there shaking, waiting for somebody to turn it into English for me, and dreading it. Every so often a word came through that I did know. Sau-kee. Shoonk-hat-tay-rah, horse. Neither of them was any comfort.", "The trouble, as I learned soon enough, was this. A fresh trail had been found near the Petit Rocher, on the Wisconsin, and the people at the villages on the Barribault were badly frightened, being certain it was the Sauks. By the look of it something like a hundred horses or more had gone by that way. Hoo-wau-ne-kah had been sent off at once to tell their Father and ask what he made of it.", "His Father heard him out and then told him the trail was almost certainly General Henry's troops, who were said to have come north hunting for the enemy. The marks of the hoofs, by his own account of them, showed the horses had been shod. That settled it. No Sauk trail would look like that. The people at the villages had nothing to be uneasy about.", "\"Very well, Father,\" said Hoo-wau-ne-kah. \"I will go back and tell my people what you say. They will believe you, for you always tell them the truth. You aren't like us Indians, who sometimes deceive each other.\" And he went back to his friends much easier in his mind than he had come.", "The picketing and the other defences were finished about then, and a detachment of troops came up from Fort Howard under Lieutenant Hunter, which between them made the fort the safest place on the Portage. So every evening, just before dusk, we formed up and marched — there is no other word for it — across to the other side of the river, to the quarters that had been given us inside the garrison, and left our own house and everything in it to the care of the Frenchmen and our friends the Winnebagoes."],
  p3s29d: ["It was on one of those days that we were sitting at the windows that look out over the Portage — we were seldom anywhere else by then, our whole occupation being to watch the road and see what was coming next — when a whoop reached us from a long way off, loud and shrill and drawn out. The news-halloo. Something had happened. We stared down the road to its farthest end and made out a body of horsemen moving toward us, and as they came on we saw they were Indians. They were in full costume. Scarlet streamers ran from the ends of their lances, and their arms threw back the sun. Nearer, and the paint came up, and the feathers, and the brooches. Fifty warriors, or more than fifty. They came to the turning that goes down to the Fort and passed it, and rode straight on up the hill to the Agency. Shaw-nee-aw-kee was not at the house. The Interpreter had been sent for the moment we first saw them at a distance, and had not yet come. The party rode to the top, halted near the blacksmith's shop, and did not get down.", "Our hearts went over. It could only be the enemy. And at that moment my husband came into view, walking up from the direction of the Interpreter's house. We called out to him to stop. He went on toward them.", "Then the chief of the party swung down off his horse, and every man behind him did the same, and they came forward to shake hands, and I could breathe again.", "They cleared a space around the leader and my husband, and the leader began to speak. He flourished a sword as he spoke and threw his arms about violently. It was the first time I had ever seen an Indian carrying that weapon, and I hated the sight of it in such hands. There were moments when the blade came around as though it were about to take my husband's head off at a stroke, and we sat there remembering the stratagems at Mackinac and Detroit in the old days. At last the speech ended. Hands were shaken again. Then we saw my husband turn and lead the way to his storehouse, and shortly his men carried out tobacco and pipes and laid them at the chief's feet.", "Our suspense was ended by being told who these people were. It was Man-Eater, the principal chief of the Rock River Indians, come in with his band to hold a talk and bring us word.", "Those Indians were under the special care of Mr. Henry Gratiot, whose efforts to keep this — the most dangerous portion of the Winnebagoes — in good feeling had been unremitting, and very shrewdly judged.", "What Man-Eater had come to tell us, and he was as noble an Indian in character as he was in appearance, only confirmed what we already had. The Sauks were working north, toward the Portage. He plainly did not know where they were."],
  p3s29e: ["Not long after Man-Eater's people had taken their leave, another party of Winnebagoes arrived, and these asked leave to dance for their Father.", "The compliment was accepted. They gathered, as they always did, on the esplanade in front of the house. My sister, the children, and I took our usual places at the open windows, and my husband sat down on the broad step at the door, the door that opens straight from the outside air into the parlor where we were.", "The dance began. They went round and round, each man following the back of the man ahead of him, and as they came past my sister, Mrs. Helm, said to me, \"Look at that small, dark Indian, with the green boughs on his person — that is a Sauk! They always mark themselves in this manner with white clay, and ornament themselves with leaves when they dance!\" I had never seen that costume on any of our own people. I looked at him properly then: green chaplets bound round his head and round his legs, and his gun wreathed the same way, and on his body no paint at all except the white transverse streaks laid over the whole of it. He was a stranger. I want to be exact, because fear will improve a face. It did not need to improve his. His face was ferocious.", "He carried the gun in his hand, and every circuit of the dance brought him directly in front of where we sat, and every time he came he turned his eyes full on us and clubbed the weapon in front of him, and what it said to us was: here I am, and what will you do. We sat still as death. To show any sign of fear would have been the worst thing we could do, and we both knew it. Then my sister said, quite low, \"I have always thought that I was to lose my life by the hands of the Indians. This is the third Indian war I have gone through, and now, I think, it will be the last.\"", "It was the only time in my life I ever saw her lose her self-possession. She was the calmest and most resolute woman I have known, and I could see now that she was trembling. And still we sat there. There was a kind of fascination in it — the more frightened we became, the less able we were to look away. Then a few drops of rain began to fall. The Indians danced on for a minute or two more, and then, all at once, with whooping and shouting, they rushed the house. We fled into my room and shut the door. My sister held it fast at first, then let go of it and came and sat beside me on the bed, because she saw that I could not get hold of myself. Of all the ways there are to die, death at the hands of savages is the hardest one to face calmly, and I believed with my whole mind that our hour had come.", "The dance never stopped. They simply carried it into the parlor and went on with it there, leaping and yelling until it seemed the roof would come down on top of us. Through a crack in the door we begged my husband and the children to come in with us. The children would not — they were having far too fine a time, and it had not entered their heads that anything was wrong. My husband came for a moment to reassure me and then judged it wisest to go back out, and stand there, and show by his presence that he was pleased by the compliment. He made light of our fears. He would not allow that the object of our suspicions was any Sauk at all, but only some young Winnebago got up in their fashion, which is a thing they sometimes do.", "It may well have been good fun to him, to go home to his village and tell how he frightened the white squaws. A white boy would do the same trick without thinking twice about it. Human nature being everywhere much the same, I do not see why it should be so unlikely in an Indian."],
  p3s30: ["The danger had come close enough by then that my husband decided to send his family to Fort Howard, which everyone believed lay well outside the enemy's range. I pleaded to be allowed to stay. It made no difference at all. He was firm.", "\"I must not leave my post,\" he said, \"while there is any danger. My departure would maybe be the signal for an immediate alliance of the Winnebagoes with the Sauks. I am certain that as long as I am here my presence will act as a restraint upon them. You wish to stay and share my dangers! Your doing so would expose us both to certain destruction in case of attack. By the aid of my friends in both tribes, I could hope to preserve my own life if I were alone, but surrounded by my family, that would be impossible — we should all fall victims together. My duty plainly is, to send you to a place of safety.\"", "The means of doing it turned up soon enough. Paquette, the Interpreter, was also an agent of the American Fur Company, and he had a boat-load of furs to send down to Green Bay, on their way to Mackinac. Mr. Kinzie had the boat made as comfortable as an open boat of that kind can be made — a tent-cloth stretched over a framework of hoop-poles across the center and lined with a dark-green blanket — put an abundant store of provisions and other comforts aboard, and gave us into the joint care of my brother Arthur and our faithful blacksmith, Mâtâ.", "Mâtâ was a tall, gaunt Frenchman with a freckled face and a great crop of crisp, sandy hair, and an incurable determination to speak English. His command of the language was not large, and he made it stranger still by putting an s on the end of nearly every word and delivering each phrase with a jerk, like a man throwing something.", "\"Davids,\" he would say to the little yellow fiddler, after an evening's frolic at the Interpreter's, \"Davids, clear away the tables and the glasses, and play fishes-hornspikes.\" He was a kind, affectionate creature, and his devotion to Monsieur Johns and Madame Johns had no limit that anyone ever found.", "Besides those two, three trusty Indians were engaged to escort us, the chief of them a man called Old Smoker. The crew of the boat were all French engagés in the service of the Fur Company — six of them, gay-hearted and merry, easing the work with their pipes and their songs, and they would have taken it as a high compliment if the ladies aboard had joined in the singing. Our hearts were too heavy that day to give them the pleasure.", "We left home on the Fourth of July, and it was a gloomy day for those of us who went and for the one who stayed. Nobody said the thing everybody was thinking, which was whether we would ever see one another again. Several of that circle had been through Indian warfare before and knew exactly what the worst looked like. And there was not even the comfort of knowing that going was safe. The Sauks might be on the other side of us. The road we were taking might — not likely, but it might — carry us straight into the middle of them. So it was a solemn leave-taking, and every one of us felt it might be a parting for this world.", "Not every one. The Frenchmen laughed and sang and cracked their jokes and assured Monsieur John that they would take Madame John and Madame Alum safe to the bay, spite of Sauks or wind or weather.", "So we set off. The Fort stayed in sight a long while, the river swinging us in toward its walls and out away from them and in again, and it was nearly mid-day before we lost the last of it and our home with it.", "At the noon-tide meal — the voyageurs measure their day in pipes — somebody made an alarming discovery. No bread had been put aboard for the crew. How it had happened, nobody could say. One man was positive a large quantity had been brought over from the garrison bakery that very morning for their use; another had seen the sacks of loaves standing in Paquette's kitchen with his own eyes. Wherever the bread was, it was not with us, and we were many miles down the river with nothing to feed six Frenchmen but some salted pork, a few beans, and some onions. We held a consultation. Should the boat turn back to the Portage for supplies? But the same danger that had put us on the water in the first place was still there, and we had been ordered to make all possible speed. On examination our own store of bread and crackers turned out to be far more than we could ever want, and we judged it would stretch to rations for the Frenchmen as far as Powell's, at the Butte des Morts, which we would reach the day after the next. We would certainly get a fresh supply there.", "That settled, we went on — and from then on we went in total silence. A song or a loud laugh was flatly forbidden until we should be past the outermost limit of the country where the enemy might be. We had been told before we started about a certain point on the river, where the low marshy meadows it had been winding through rise into firmer, higher ground. That is the border of the Menomonee territory. It is also the place where the Sauks, if they had crossed north of the Wisconsin and were making for the Chippewa country, would most likely be met."],
  p3s31: ["On the forenoon of the second day word came back along the boat that we were coming up on that place. I will not pretend to anything braver than the truth: we held our breath for awe.", "The three Winnebagoes were up in the bow. Old Smoker, the chief, sat squatted on his feet on the bench of the foremost rowers. We watched him instead of the river. He was staring hard at the wooded point ahead of us, and we followed the line of his eyes, and while we were looking three Indians stepped out of the trees and stood on the bank. We said it to each other under our breath: if they are Sauks, we are lost, because the whole body of them will be in that thicket. The boat went on. Nobody spoke. There was the dip of the paddle, and there was, I think, the sound of some hearts, and nothing else at all. We looked back at the chief. His nostrils were flared open and his eyes were almost glaring.", "Then he came off that bench in a single bound and let out his long, shrill whoop.", "\"Hoh! hoh! hoh! Neechee — friend — Muh-no-mo-nee!\"", "After that it was all gladness. Every one of us was pressing forward to shake hands with the strangers the moment we could reach them, purely out of joy that they were Menomonees and not the others. They could tell us nothing whatever about the Sauks.", "By noon that day we counted ourselves clear of the dangerous country. We were still careful. When the boat was pushed ashore for the mid-day pipe, under a fine overhanging bank with thick woods on top of it, the watchfulness slackened a little, and the young people were let out to wander a short way from the boat with Arthur and Mâtâ minding them.", "They came back at a run. The woods, they said, were alive with pigeons — you could nearly knock them down with a stick — and would we please, please let them shoot just enough for supper. No. The enemy might be closer than we thought, and one shot fired would tell them exactly where we were, and the wise thing was to give notice to nobody, friend or foe. They went back to the boat without their game, and not happily.", "The next morning brought us to Powell's, at the Butte des Morts. There was not a loaf of bread in the place, and the faces of the poor Frenchmen when they heard it were a hard thing to look at. Our own store was finished by then too. All that could be got as a substitute was a bag of dark, bitter-looking flour. That was the provision for our whole party, and we had to be content with it, and we left the Hillock of the Dead feeling that it had been the grave of our hopes as well."],
  p3s31a: ["Hard rowing brought us before long to the place where the river runs out into that beautiful sheet of water, Winnebago Lake. There was hardly any wind when we came onto it, but the Frenchmen hoisted their sail anyway, in hope of being spared the labor of rowing across. Then they whistled for a wind. They whistled with the whole force of their lungs and nothing came of it. Then they appealed to La Vierge, in that comical mixture of joking and real reverence they keep for such occasions, and nothing came of that either. At last somebody proposed the only remaining course: the goddess of the winds must be propitiated with an offering of some cast-off garment.", "Guardapié, who spoke for the crew in all matters, made the application all round. Not one of the poor voyageurs owned a spare article of any kind. A few old rags were finally dug out of the little hold in the bow where they keep their food and their clothing and their dirt in one heap, and these were thrown into the waves. For a moment everybody believed it had worked. The sail stirred, filled a little — and then flapped down flat against the mast and hung there, dead as a rag on a line. The party were in despair. Then Julian and Edwin put their heads together, whispered, and stepped forward as messengers of mercy. In a trice they had their jackets and vests off and were offering the crew the next garment in, for the raising of the wind.", "The boatmen hesitated. It seemed to them too magnificent a gift to accept. But a preparatory shake or two revealed that the contribution was worth rather less than they had supposed, and with a great shout of laughter they gave it to the waves. To the enormous delight and astonishment of the boys, a breeze got up at that exact moment and carried the little vessel beautifully along, about half of the way to Garlic Island. There the charm ran out. It could not be renewed. Further offerings of the same kind produced nothing at all, and every other expedient failed too, and the Frenchmen went back to their oars with some distinctly disrespectful remarks about the lady whose help they had asked for.", "Two or three canoes of Winnebagoes came in at the island at the same time we did, and their owners came straight up with an offering of sturgeon they had taken in the lake. As this promised a pleasant change for the noon meal — for the Frenchmen at least — we decided to stop and kindle a fire and cook it. We used the interval to send the boys off to the far side of the island, where the water is clear and shallow over a pebbly beach and altogether too tempting to pass up. Harry, the black boy, went along to watch them bathe, and we amused ourselves picking the fine red raspberries that grow all over that island.", "Our enjoyment was cut short by a discovery. The whole place — the vines, the shrubs, and by every appearance the ground itself — was crawling with wood-ticks. The wood-tick fastens on the skin and then works its way into the flesh, and what it leaves behind is a swelling and an irritation that is exceedingly painful and can be dangerous. We raised the alarm and got the boys back at speed to the open, well-trodden part of the island. But we had not left our tormentors behind us. We were conscious of them all that day. They were not done with us for several days after, and the ones that had managed to bury themselves had to be taken out with the point of a penknife or a large needle. When dinner was over we boarded the boat, and the wind having risen in earnest at last, it carried us down to the far verge of the lake and into the mouth of the river beyond — the Winnebago Rapids, they call that stretch.", "On the point of land to the right stood a cluster of neat bark wigwams. That was Four-Legs' village, and we came down on it with the current running under us."],
  p3s31b: ["The run down the rapids was a lively business and not entirely a safe one, and at the end of it came the Grand Chûte, a fall of several feet, which the boat went over and came out of right side up. Toward evening we reached the settlement of the Waubanakees at the head of the Little Chûte. These are the Stockbridge, or Brothertown, Indians — what is left of the old Mohicans — who had come out a few years before from Oneida County, in the State of New York, to a tract the United States granted them on the fertile banks of the Fox River. They had already cut wide openings into the forest and put up substantial, comfortable houses near the water, where the banks stand high and carry, for most of their length, enormous trees.", "We decided to ask these people for hospitality, though only a corner of somebody's fire, enough to boil our tea-kettle and bake the short-cake that had stood in for bread for nearly two days now. The making of that cake had been the great joke of the trip. Powell's black flour, some salt, and a little butter went into the tin box that had held our meat; the box was then turned over, and scoured, and became the dough-board; the vinegar-bottle was the rolling-pin; a shallow tin dish did for the baking. The Waubanakees were good enough to lend us an iron bake-kettle as well, and to stand over the cake and see it properly cooked, once Harry had carried it up to their house.", "They were so kind about all of it that the crew took the notion of asking for a lodging on shore — three nights packed into that boat had been enough for them. Arthur and Mâtâ came round to the same idea shortly after, and we were pressed to do likewise, with the assurance that the houses were extremely neat and orderly.", "We chose to stay aboard. It was a fine night and everything in the boat had been arranged so comfortably, and we kept Edwin and Josette with us.", "The boat was moored tight, because the Little Chûte lay just below us, and a craft that broke loose in that current and went down over the falls would be a very serious matter indeed. As a further precaution one man was left aboard to keep everything safe and in order. Then the rest of them climbed the bank and went off to their beds in the Waubanakee cabins.", "It was a beautiful, calm, moonlit night, the air just warm enough to be pleasant, and the low steady murmur of the rapids and the fall not far off put our party to sleep in no time. How long we slept I could not say. What woke us was a rush of wind. It caught the poles holding up the awning and bent them, and then snapped them, and a second gust took the tent and the blanket clean off the boat and away downstream. The moonlight was gone. Then a flash of lightning showed them to us, once, far off down the water, sailing away like a spectre in the distance."],
  p3s31c: ["The storm got worse. The rain came down in torrents, and the thunder and the lightning began to come one on top of the other with a speed that was frightening. My sister went for the Frenchman to wake him. \"Get up, Vitelle, quick,\" she cried, in French. \"Run up the bank for Mâtâ and Mr. Arthur — tell them to come and get us instantly.\"", "He did not answer her. He went down on his knees instead and began calling on the Virgin at the top of his voice.", "\"Don't wait for the Virgin, but go as quickly as possible. Do you not see we shall all be killed?\"", "\"Oh! not for the world, madame, not for the world,\" said Vitelle, pushing his head into a pack of furs, \"would I go up that bank in this storm.\" And from inside the furs he set about crying to every saint in the calendar.", "It was awful. The roar of the thunder and the flash of the lightning all round us were like a park of artillery firing without stopping. I got my cloak out, with some difficulty, and wrapped myself and Josette in it. Sister Margaret did the same for Edwin.", "\"Oh, madame,\" said the poor little girl, her teeth going with cold and fright, \"won't we be drowned?\"", "\"Very well,\" said my sister to the Frenchman. \"You see that Madame John is at the last agony — if you won't go for help I must, and Monsieur John must know that you left his wife to perish.\"", "That was more than Vitelle could carry. \"If I must, I must,\" he said, and with one desperate bound he was over the side and onto the shore and going up that hill for all he was worth.", "It was only a few minutes before a whole posse came flying down the bank, though it felt like an age from where we sat. The lightning was so constant that everything showed as plainly as in daylight. Mâtâ's curly hair was standing straight up off his head, and his eyes rolled at the sight of us with a kind of ghastly astonishment.", "\"Oh, my God, Madame Johns! what would Monsieur Johns say, to see you nows?\" he cried, and got me up in his arms and carried me up the hill. Arthur came behind with sister Margaret, and two of the others had Edwin and Josette. Nobody carried Vitelle. He had been careful not to risk his precious life by going down to the boat a second time.", "At the cabin where Arthur and Mâtâ had been lodged, a fire was got going, though not easily, and our trunks were brought up from the boat, and at last we were able to get out of our drenched clothes and the children out of theirs and into something better. Then we lay down on the clean, homely bed and slept until daylight."],
  p3s31d: ["We had to find out how far the water had got into the cargo of furs, so an early start was proposed. The people in the cottages, however, had grown weary of doing good. They declined to make us any breakfast, though we assured them they would be well paid for their trouble. So we saw ourselves compelled to leave with very slender prospects of a morning meal.", "When we came down to the boat, we stopped where we stood. Bedclothes, cloaks, trunks, the mess-basket, the packs of furs — every one of them carried the mark of a complete deluge. The boat lay ankle-deep in water. There was literally no place on board where a person could either stand or sit. We bailed for a while. We dragged out the packs that had suffered least and arranged them across the center to make a sort of divan, and when that was done nothing better seemed to offer than to climb back in and endure what could not be cured.", "Our position was not an enviable one. Wherever a foot or a hand went down, the water came up around it with a bubbling sound, and the state of the bandboxes and work-baskets does not bear describing. Breakfast there was none. When we opened the mess-basket, everything in it had been mingled into one undistinguishable mass — tea, pepper, salt, short-cake, all floating together. It was a hopeless case.", "That was not the worst of it. As the fervid July sun climbed higher, the steam that came off every object on board was nearly suffocating. The boat was old. The packs of skins were old. Their company on a dry day had been anything but agreeable. Now it was intolerable, and there was no retreating from it, no corner of the boat where the air was any different. So we encouraged the children to arm themselves with patience for the short time that yet remained of our voyage.", "Seated on our odoriferous couch, beneath the shade of a single umbrella that had to protect our whole party from the scorching sun, we glided wearily down the stream through that long, tedious day. We passed the Kakalin, then the Rapids, then Dickenson's, then the Agency. With what longing eyes we watched those human habitations go by, where other people were enjoying the shelter of a roof and the comforts of food — and how eagerly we counted the hours that must pass before we could reach Fort Howard.", "There were no songs from the poor Frenchmen that day. Music and fasting do not go well together. In time we stopped at Shanty-town, where the boat was to be unloaded, and every hand fell to work transferring the cargo up to the warehouse of the Fur Company, which stood near the landing. It was not a long operation, for they all worked heartily. This being accomplished, the voyageurs, one and all, prepared to take their leave. In vain Mâtâ stormed and raved. In vain Arthur remonstrated.", "\"No,\" they said. They had brought the boat and cargo to the warehouse. That was all of their job. And they turned to go.", "\"Guardapié,\" I said, \"do you intend to leave us here?\"", "\"Bien, madame! It is the place we always stop at.\"", "\"Does Monsieur John pay you for bringing his family down?\"", "\"Oh, yes. Monsieur John has given us an order on the sutler, at the Fort down below.\"", "\"To be paid when you deliver us safe at the Fort down below. It seems I shall be there before you, and I shall arrange that matter. Monsieur John never dreamed that this would be your conduct.\"", "The Frenchmen drew together and consulted, and the result was that Guardapié and two others climbed into the boat, took up their oars, and rowed us, rather sulkily, the remaining two miles to Fort Howard."],
  p3s32: ["It did not take us long to find out that Green Bay had worked itself into a panic over the Sauks. The town had settled on a conviction and would not be talked out of it: the enemy was coming here, on their way to Canada, to put themselves under the protection of the British Government. By what route they were to manage the last leg of it, nobody could say. Whether they meant to halt on the shore and build themselves bark canoes for the crossing, or whether they intended to charter one of Mr. Newbery's schooners, the good people had not quite settled in their minds. What was beyond dispute was that a share of the citizens had frightened themselves nearly to death, and had decided there was no safety anywhere on earth except behind the walls of the old fort — a fort falling apart at the seams, and emptied besides, since nearly every soldier in it had been pulled out and marched off to Fort Winnebago some time before.", "Then a traveler came through with a story, and the story did the rest. He had, he said, slept one night on the exact ground where the Sauks took their breakfast the next morning. Everyone knew the Sauks were down to very short commons by then. It followed, to the town's satisfaction, that half an hour's delay on his part would have seen him eaten, and the man was accordingly credited with a wonderful escape.", "Our own friends and acquaintances took no part in any of it. Anyone willing to think for a moment about the country such a march would have to cross, and about the numerous and well-armed whites waiting at the far end of it, could see the thing was impossible. Still, there were people no argument could reach, who believed safety lay in flight and nowhere else, and as the anxiety wound tighter the eyes of the town kept turning to the water, watching for the \"Mariner.\""],
  p3s33: ["The \"Mariner\" came in at last, early, with the light still low on the water. Within an hour of her tying up, every house from one end of the settlement to the other had what she carried — \"the cholera was in this country! It was in Detroit - it was among the troops who were on their way to the seat of war! Whole companies had died of it in the river St. Clair, and the survivors had been put on shore at Port Gratiot, to save their lives as best they might!\" A savage enemy on one side of us and a pestilence on the other, and no ground between them to stand on.", "Anyone with people in the East took it hardest. It went through the place and left men hollowed out. The first to bring it to us was an officer whose courage no one had ever thought to question — he had proved it on the battle-field and proved it again in private ventures that asked more daring than most men have in them. He got the words out. Then he laid his head against the window-sill and wept like a child.", "Those who had to reach people near and dear to them went out again aboard the \"Mariner.\" The rest of us judged that where we already stood was the safer place, and we were right: the scourge never came to Green Bay that season.", "The heat that summer was punishing, and the mosquitoes came in such numbers that none of us so much as pretended to walk the parade after sundown without two fans, or green branches, kept swinging every moment to drive them off — which is, as it happens, the surest way in the world to bring them on. We had somehow let ourselves forget the flat indifference of Old Smoker, which used to astonish us: he would sit without moving and let his bare arms and body go literally gray with them, and then wipe off a handful, the blood following the track of his hand across the skin, and settle back and stoically wait for the chance to do it again. They say a mosquito left undisturbed until he has taken his fill leaves a far less inflamed bite than one brushed away in the middle of his feast.", "The daylight hours belonged to what is called the Green Bay fly, a species of dragon-fly. There are days when the outer walls of the houses carry so many that you cannot tell what color the wall beneath them is. Their lives are ephemeral things, scarcely more than a day long. Indoors you find the bodies stuck fast to the walls and the window-glass; outdoors they fall in such quantity that once a high wind has swept them into long rows against the sides of the quarters, a person can walk down them and toss them up underfoot like dry leaves in autumn."],
  p3s34: ["When we walked across the parade, our attention was sometimes caught by a tapping on the bars of the dungeon where a criminal was confined. It was the murderer of Lieutenant Foster.", "It may be remembered that this amiable young officer had been our traveling companion on the trip from Chicago the year before. Some months after he reached Fort Howard, he had occasion to order a soldier of his company, a man named Doyle, into confinement for intoxication. A few days later Doyle persuaded the sergeant of the guard to walk him over to Lieutenant Foster's quarters, on the plea that he wished to speak with him. He went up the stairs to the young officer's room. The sergeant and another soldier stayed below at the foot of them, near the door.", "Doyle came in and said, \"Will you please tell me, lieutenant, what I am confined for?\"", "\"No, sir,\" the officer replied. \"You know your offense well enough. Return to your place of confinement.\"", "The man ran back down the stairs, wrenched the gun out of the sergeant's hands, came up again, and discharged it into Lieutenant Foster's heart.", "Foster turned toward his inner apartment. He said, \"Ah me!\" and fell dead in the doorway.", "Doyle had been tried by a civil court and was now under sentence, waiting to be executed. He was a hardened villain. He never showed the slightest compunction for what he had done.", "The commanding officer, Major Clark, sent to him one day to ask whether he wanted anything for his comfort.", "\"If the Major pleased,\" he answered, \"he should like to have a light and a copy of Byron's Works.\"", "There were some fears that he would find a way to make away with himself before the day of execution, and to guard against it he was stripped of everything that might furnish him a weapon. His food was served to him in a wooden bowl, in case a shard of broken crockery should give him the means of self-destruction.", "One morning he sent a little package to the commanding officer as a present. Inside was a strong rope, made out of strips of his blanket that he had patiently separated, with a large stout spike fastened at the end of it. The message that came with it was that he wished Major Clark to see that if he chose to put an end to himself, he could find the means to do it in spite of him.", "That hardened frame of mind held to the last. As he was led out for execution, passing beyond the gate, he noticed a quantity of lumber lately collected there for the construction of a new Company's warehouse.", "\"Ah, captain, what are you going to build here?\" he asked of Captain Scott, who was attending him.", "\"Doyle,\" his captain replied, \"you have but a few moments to live. You had better employ your thoughts about something else.\"", "\"It is for that very reason, captain,\" he said, \"that I am inquiring. As my time is short, I wish to gain all the information I can while it lasts.\""],
  p3s35: ["We were not left long in suspense about the friends we had left behind. In less than two weeks Old Smoker appeared again. He carried letters from my husband, telling me that General Dodge was then with him at Fort Winnebago, that Generals Henry and Alexander were at the Fort as well, and that as soon as they had recruited their men and horses — which were pretty well worn out with scouring the country after Black Hawk — they would march again in pursuit of him toward the head-waters of the Rock River, where they had every reason to believe, from information the Winnebagoes had lately brought in, that he would be found.", "Since he charged us to lay aside all uneasiness on his account, and held out the hope of coming for us soon, or of sending for us, our minds grew more tranquil than they had been in some time.", "Not long after that, I was told one morning that \"a lady\" wished to see me at the front door. I obeyed the summons, and there on the step, to my surprise, stood my friend Madame Four-Legs. She made much demonstration of joy at seeing me — putting her two hands together over her forehead and then parting them in a waving kind of gesture, laughing, patting me on the arms — and then drew from her bosom a letter from my husband, which she had carried to me. It read to this effect: \"Generals Dodge and Henry left here a few days since, accompanied by Paquette, they met the Sauks near the Wisconsin, on the 21st. A battle ensued, in which upwards of fifty of the enemy were killed - our loss was one killed, and eight wounded. The citizens are well pleased that all this has been accomplished without any aid from Old White Beaver. [56] The war must be near its close, for the militia and regulars together will soon finish the remaining handful of fugitives.\""],
  p3s36: ["Lieutenant Hunter arrived with leave of absence in order to escort us, and after that everything fell into train for our return to Fort Winnebago. There was no Mackinac boat to be had. In its place we got a Durham boat, which is longer and shallower than the other, with no way of rigging an awning or any shelter over the center of it. What it did have was size, and size suited a party like ours, which counted, besides our own family, Lieutenant and Mrs. Hunter, the wife of another officer now stationed at Fort Winnebago, and our cousin, Miss Forsyth. We made up our minds, as may be supposed, to pretty close quarters.", "Our crew was made up partly of Frenchmen and partly of soldiers, and, all things being ready, we set off on a fine bright morning in the latter part of July.", "The second day of alternate rowing and poling brought us to the Grande Chûte early in the afternoon.", "The custom here is to put everyone ashore at the foot of the rapids and let them climb the high bank and walk around the fall, while the men haul the boat up through the foaming water.", "Most of our party had already stepped out onto the shore when a sudden thought took hold of one of the ladies and myself.", "\"Let us stay in the boat,\" we said, \"and be pulled up the Chûte.\" The rest of the company went on up the bank. We sat where we were and watched the men make their preparations with great interest. They were over the side and into the water in no time, and they made a strong rope fast to the bow and all lent their weight to it, marching slowly forward with their heavy load behind them. The cargo, which was only our trunks and our stores, and of no very considerable weight, had not been taken out.", "Up we went, taking now and then a tremendous bump against a rock we never saw, and often catching a shower of foam as the water roared and boiled around us.", "The men kept as close as they could to the high, sheer bank, where the water ran smoothest. At the head of the cordel was a merry simpleton of a Frenchman who kept turning his head to grin back at us, delighted with how plainly we were enjoying ourselves.", "We were in high glee. \"Isn't this charming?\" one of us cried. \"I only wish — \"", "The wish, whatever it was going to be, was cut off by a shout and a crash. \"Have a care, Robineau! Mind where you are taking the boat!\" But the warning came too late. Being more occupied with the ladies than with his duty, the leader had walked us straight into a sharp, projecting tree that hung out from the bank. The first tug tore the side out of the boat, and the water came in at once.", "My companion and I jumped for the nearest rocks that showed their heads above the foam. Our screams and the shouting of the men brought Lieutenant Hunter and some Indians down off the bank at a run. They carried us in their arms to land, while the men worked lustily at fishing up the contents of the boat, every article of it now thoroughly soaked.", "We scrambled up the high bank in a miserable state, to join the general lamentation over what the accident had probably cost us.", "\"Oh! my husband's new uniform!\" cried one, and, \"Oh! the miniatures in the bottom of my trunk!\" sighed another, while, \"Oh! the silk dresses, and the ribbons, and the finery!\" made up the general chorus.", "Nobody thought about the provisions, though we had all seen, on our way to shore, the barrel of bread and the tub of ice that Lieutenant Hunter had so providently brought for our food and drink, sailing off down the dancing waves. Among the boxes carried to land and toted up the steep bank was one holding loaves of sugar and packages of tea, which I had bought from the sutler at the post for our winter's supply. The young Indian who had carried it set it down on the ground, and soon called my attention to a thick white stream oozing out of the corners. I made signs for him to taste it. He dipped his finger in, and when he understood what it was he called out to his companions with delight. I pointed then at his hatchet, and motioned him to open the box. He did not require a second invitation. It was hacked to pieces in no time.", "Then I beckoned up all the rest of the youngsters, who were standing about full of wonder, and there followed such a scrambling and shouting that we all went into fits of laughter, the boys worst of all. Bowls, dippers, hands — everything that could hold even the smallest quantity was put in requisition. The squaws were the most active of any. Those who could do no better took the stoutest fragments of the blue paper the sugar had been wrapped in, and in a trice there was nothing left but the wet yellow bundles of tea and the splinters of the box that had held it.", "By this time fires had been made, and the articles out of the trunks were soon spread over every shrub and bush in the vicinity. Fortunately the box with the new uniform in it had been piled high above the others, in the center of the boat, and had taken very little damage. The state of the wardrobes in general was sad.", "Not a white article was to be seen anywhere. Everything was mottled — blue, green, red and black running into one another in streaks, and dripping from the ends and corners.", "To add to the trouble, the rain began to fall, as rain will at an inconvenient moment, and before long the half-dried garments had to be gathered out of the smoke and huddled away in a most discouraging condition.", "The tent was pitched wet as it was, and the blankets, wrung out and only partly dried, were spread on the ground for us to lie on that night.", "A Hamburg cheese, which had been part of my stores, was voted to me for a pillow, and after a supper whose best feature was a portion of one of the wet loaves — it had stayed in a barrel wedged too tightly to drift away — we betook ourselves to our repose."],
  p3s37: ["The next morning came up hot and sultry. The mosquitoes, which the rain had kept off through the night, began to make up their losses, and they tormented us unmercifully.", "After a breakfast that was both uncomfortable and unpalatable, the first question was what we were to do with ourselves. Our boat lay under water at the foot of the hill, half-way up the rapids. The nearest home among the Waubanakees was some miles off, and there was no way of reaching it except by an Indian canoe, if any of our present friends and neighbors would be obliging enough to bring one for our use. Even then it was doubtful whether boats enough could be found to carry all of our numerous party back to Green Bay.", "In the middle of these perplexing consultations, a whoop came from beyond the hill, which slopes away to the north there at the head of the rapids.", "\"There is John! That is certainly his voice!\" cried more than one of the company.", "It was my husband, and in a moment he was standing among us. Never was an arrival more opportune, or more plainly providential.", "He had not learned our plans — the unsettled state of the country had kept us from getting word to him — and he had come provided with a boat, to take us back to Fort Winnebago.", "Our drying operations, which we had started again that morning, were cut short. Everything was shuffled away as fast as hands could do it, and in an incredibly short time we had been transferred to the other boat, which lay quietly above the Chûte, and were pulling away toward Winnebago Lake.", "We had resolved to go no farther than the neighborhood of the lake, where the breeze would make the mosquitoes less intolerable, and then to stop and make one more attempt at drying our clothing. So when we reached a beautiful high bank near the Little Butte, we stopped for that purpose, unpacked the trunks again, and soon every bush and twig was fluttering with the spoils of the cruel waves.", "We had hardly disposed of the last rag and ribbon when we heard the tramp of horses, and then loud shouts and cheering ringing through the forest.", "A company of about twenty-five horsemen came riding into the middle of us, banners flying, veils streaming from their hats, arms glittering in the sun. They wanted, amid greetings and roars of laughter, to know the nature and the reasons of our singular state of confusion.", "It was Colonel Stambough and Alexander Irwin, of Green Bay, with a company of young volunteers and a whooping band of Menomonees behind them, all bound for the seat of war. We comforted them with the assurance that by this time the victories were all won and the scalps taken. They expressed a hope that there were yet a few laurels to be earned, bade us adieu, and pushed on rapidly with their march.", "We crossed Lake Winnebago by the clear, beautiful light of a summer moon. The soft air was just enough to swell the sail, and so spare the men their labor at the oar.", "The witchery of the hour was not, however, enough to make us give up our rest after the heat and the annoyances of the day, and so we disposed ourselves betimes to be packed away in the center of the boat. How it was accomplished, not one of that numerous company could have told you. If any accident had happened to disturb the arrangement, I am sure it would have been a Chinese puzzle to get us all back into our places. The men on the outside had much the best of it, and we rather envied those who were off watch their ability to snore and change position as the humor took them.", "We reached Powell's just in time to have gone ashore and made our breakfast, if we had had anything to make it out of. We had hoped to get supplies here, having lived until now on what remained of my husband's ample stock. That stock was so nearly gone that when we found the mess-basket could not be replenished at this place, we began to talk of putting ourselves on allowance.", "The wet bread, of which there was still an ample store, had, as may readily be imagined, fermented under a July sun. The tea, in spite of our careful efforts at drying it on newspapers and pieces of board, had gone musty and was unfit for use. There was literally nothing left except the salted meat and a few crackers, hardly enough for the present day.", "So the men were urged to make all the speed they could, that we might reach Gleason's, at Lake Puckaway, in good season the following day.", "In the evening, when we stopped to take our tea at a beautiful little opening among the trees, we found our old enemies, the mosquitoes, worse than ever. We had to put on cloaks and gloves and tie our veils close around our throats, and only ventured to introduce a cracker or a cup of tea under this protection in the most stealthy manner.", "The men rowed well and brought us to Gleason's about eleven o'clock the next day. We were greeted with the most enthusiastic demonstrations by my old friend La Grosse Américaine, who had come here from Bellefontaine.", "\"Oh, Mrs. Armstrong,\" we cried, \"get us some breakfast — we are famishing!\"", "Then who should appear but our faithful Mâtâ, driving the old calèche in which we were in the habit of making our little excursions in the neighborhood of the Fort. He had ridden over hoping to meet us, on the idea that some of us would prefer this way of reaching home.", "With provident thoughtfulness, he had brought tea, roasted coffee, fresh butter, eggs, and the like, in case we should be short of such luxuries at that advanced stage of our trip.", "His \"Good-morning, Madame Johns! How do you dos?\" was a pleasant and a welcome sound.", "We could not wait for our breakfast, but gathered around La Grosse Américaine like a parcel of children while she cut and spread slices of bread-and-butter for us.", "When the regular meal was over, it was decided that sister Margaret should take Josette and go back with Mâtâ to open the house and make it ready to receive us. It had been the head-quarters of militia, Indians and stragglers of various descriptions the whole time we were gone, and we could easily imagine that a little misrule and unreason might have had its way there for that period.", "We had seventy-two miles yet to go, by the devious winding course of the river — first over the beautiful waters of Lac de Boeuf, and then through the low marshy lands that spread away to the Portage. One of the gentlemen made an attempt to work up a little excitement among the ladies as we came near the place where it was thought the Sauks might cross on their way to the Chippewa country.", "\"Who knows,\" he said, gravely, \"but they may be lurking in this neighborhood yet? If so, we shall probably have some signal. We must be on the alert!\"", "Some of the ladies began to turn pale and look about them. Then came an interval of perfect silence, and out of it a low, prolonged whistle. There was so much agitation, and even actual terror, that the mischievous author of the trick had to confess on the spot, and take a hearty scolding for the pain he had caused.", "Just before sunset of the second day from Gleason's, we reached our home. Everything was radiant with neatness and good order. With the efficient help of our good Manaigre and his wife, the house had been whitewashed from the roof down to the door-sill, thoroughly scrubbed and cleansed, the carpets unpacked and spread upon the floors, the furniture arranged, and, last but not least, a noble supper was smoking on the board by the time we had made, once more, a civilized toilet.", "Many of our friends from the Fort were there to greet us, and a happier or more thankful party has seldom been gathered anywhere."],
  p3s38: ["By then everyone was calling the war over. Word had reached us of the Bad Axe, where the regulars, the militia and the steamboat Warrior together had made a finish of the handful of Sauks still left, and with the news the hearts and the houses of the frontier settlers came back to rest.", "It may seem strange that an enemy so few in number, with so little to fight with, could throw a whole country into a panic and take such an enormous force to put down. The whole of the trouble was that nobody ever knew where to find them, either to strike at them or to guard against them. At the outset, I suspect, every military man in the territory felt exactly as that fine old veteran General Brady felt. \"Give me two infantry companies mounted,\" he said, \"and I will engage to whip the Sauks out of the country in one week!\"", "True enough. But before you can whip an enemy you have to meet him, and to follow Indians to any purpose and take them requires a particular kind of schooling — one that in those days hardly a man could claim, the frontier militia included.", "Through parts of that campaign there was a second trouble: the two branches of the service would not act as one. The regulars looked down on the militia's unprofessional way of going at things; the militia jeered at the slow, empty formalities of the regulars. Each side held it as an article of faith that the work would go better with the other out of it, and the militia, being quick to move, now and then took the business into their own hands and produced defeat and disgrace, as they did in the affair called \"Stillman's Run.\"", "The contempt the army officers felt for the militia ran downhill to their servants and dependants. The Ranger officers had paid their visit to Fort Winnebago before the battle of the Wisconsin, and afterward the officer of the mess that had entertained them called up his servant one day to go through the sutler's accounts. It was the same small \"Yellow David\" who had formerly been Captain Harney's.", "\"David,\" said the young gentleman, \"I see three bottles of cologne-water charged in the month's account of the mess at the sutler's. What does that mean?\"", "\"If you please, lieutenant,\" said David, respectfully, \"it was to sweeten up the dining-room and quarters after them milish' officers were here visiting.\""],
  p3s39: ["Black Hawk got away northward with a handful of his warriors. It bought him little time. The One-eyed Day-kau-ray and his party ran him down and delivered him as a prisoner to General Street at Prairie du Chien. The women and the children of his band had already been dealt with another way — loaded into canoes and pushed off down the Mississippi, on the hope that somebody would let them cross over and rejoin the rest of their tribe.", "Somebody had lashed the canoes to one another. That was the detail that mattered. On the current they went over, one dragging the next, and the children went into the river, and their mothers were too starved and too spent to go in after them. Those who lived through it were taken prisoner and brought up to Prairie du Chien, hungry and past caring. Our mother was at the Port when they came in. She had never seen people brought so low. One of the women had a little Chippewa, and with it she laid out what the band had endured. There is no softening it. It was appalling.", "They had eaten the horses they could spare. After the horses it was acorns, and elm-bark, and at the end of it grass. Some stopped and died where they stopped, and the whites coming on behind found the bodies lying along the trail. The woman who spoke to our mother had lost her husband in the fighting and every one of her children in the canoe that went over, and the only thing she wanted now was to go where they had gone. Poor Indians! who can wonder that they don't love the whites?"],
  p3s40: ["We had been quietly at home only a very short time when a summons came for my husband to collect the principal chiefs of the Winnebagoes and meet General Scott and Governor Reynolds at Rock Island, where it was proposed to hold a treaty for the purchase of all the lands east and south of the Wisconsin. Messengers went out to gather the principal men, and, accompanied by as many of them as chose to report themselves, he set off on his trip.", "He had been gone about two weeks, and I was beginning to count the days that must pass before I could reasonably look for him back, when one afternoon I walked over to pay a visit to my sister at the Fort. As I came into the large hall of one range of quarters, Lieutenant Lacy came in suddenly from the opposite direction, and, hardly stopping, called out, \"Bad news, madam! Have you heard it?\"", "\"No. What is it?\"", "\"The cholera has broken out at Rock Island, and they are dying by five hundred a day. Dr. Finley has just arrived with the news.\" And he was gone, without waiting to answer a question.", "The cholera at Rock Island. And my husband there. I flew to the other door of the hall, the one that looks out on the parade-ground. A sentinel was walking near it. \"Soldier,\" I cried, \"will you run to the young officers' quarters and ask Dr. Finley to come here for a moment?\"", "The man shook his head. He was not allowed to leave his post.", "Then Mrs. Lacy's servant-girl came out of a door under the steps. She was a worthless creature, but where help was so scarce, ladies could not afford to keep a scrupulous tariff of moral qualification.", "\"Oh! Catharine,\" I said, \"will you run over and ask Dr. Finley to come here a moment? I must hear what news he has brought from Rock Island.\" She put on a modest look and said, \"I don't like to go to the young officers' quarters.\"", "I was indignant at her hypocrisy. I was also wild with impatience — and then, to my great joy, Dr. Finley himself appeared.", "\"Where is my husband?\" I cried.", "\"On his way home, madam, safe and sound. He will probably be here tomorrow.\" He then gave me an account of the ravages the cholera was making among the troops. They were severe, though less so than rumor had first proclaimed them.", "For all the doctor's assurance of his safety, my husband was seized with cholera on the trip. By the kind care of Paquette, and the plentiful use of chicken-broth administered to him by the poor woman at whose cabin he had stopped, he recovered, and reached his home in safety, having taken Prairie du Chien on his route and brought his mother back with him again to her home.", "The Indians had consented to the sale of their beautiful domain. Indeed, in such cases there is no alternative. If they persist in keeping their lands, and become surrounded and hemmed in by the white settlers, their situation is more deplorable than if they had surrendered their homes altogether. They are aware of this, and so, as a general thing, they give up their lands when the Government proposes it, and only take care to make the best bargain for themselves that they can. In this instance they were to receive as an equivalent a tract of land[57] running into the interior of Iowa, and an additional sum of ten thousand dollars a year.", "One of the stipulations of the treaty was that the Winnebagoes should surrender certain people of their tribe who were accused of having taken part with the Sauks in some of the murders on the frontier, so that they might be tried by our laws, and acquitted or punished as the case might be."],
  p3s41: ["Wau-kaun-kah, the Little Snake, offered himself as a hostage until the suspected people should be delivered up. He was received as such by the Agent, and marched over and put in confinement at the Fort, to stay there until the seven accused men came in to redeem him.", "It took the nation some little time to persuade those men to place themselves in the hands of the whites, so that they might receive justice according to the laws of the whites. The trial of Red Bird, and his slow death in prison afterward, were still fresh in their memories. It took a good deal of resolution, and a strong conviction of their own innocence, to brace them up to such a step.", "It had to be brought about by argument and by persuasion. The nation would never have resorted to force to compel the fulfilment of their own stipulation.", "In the meantime a solemn talk was held with the principal chiefs, assembled at the Agency. A great part of the nation was in the immediate neighborhood, in obedience to a notice sent out by Governor Porter, who, by virtue of his office as Governor of Michigan Territory, was also Superintendent of the Northwest Division of the Indians. Instead of calling on the Agent to take charge of the annuity money, as had been the custom until then, the Governor had announced that he would bring it himself to Fort Winnebago and be present at the payment. The appointed time had now come, and with it the main body of the Winnebagoes.", "Those of the Indians who had not attended the treaty at Rock Island, and had no hand in the ceding of their country, were loud in their condemnation of the step and in their lamentations over it. Foremost among them was Wild-Cat, the Falstaff of Garlic Island and its vicinity. It was little wonder that he should shed bitter tears, as he did, over the loss of his beautiful home on the blue waters of Winnebago Lake.", "If he had not been accidentally stopped on his way to the treaty, he said, and detained until it was too late, he would never, never have permitted the bargain.", "His Father, who knew that the real cause of his failing to accompany his countrymen to Rock Island was a desperate frolic he had been enticed into along the way, replied gravely that he had heard of the chief's misfortune on this occasion. How, in ascending the Fox River, a couple of kegs of whiskey had come floating down the stream, and had run foul of his canoe with such force that the canoe was injured, and he had been obliged to stop several days at the Mee-kan to repair damages.", "The shouts of laughter that greeted this explanation were so contagious that poor Wild-Cat was compelled to join in and treat his misfortune as a joke.", "The suspected Indians engaged the services of Judge Doty to defend them at their future trial, and in time notice was given that on a certain day they would be brought to the Portage and surrendered to their Father, who would transfer them to the keeping of the military officer appointed to receive them.", "It was joyful news to poor Wau-kaun-kah that the day of his release was at hand. Every time we had been inside the walls of the Fort, we had been saluted by a call from him, from his station at the guard-room window.", "\"Do you hear anything of those Indians? When are they coming, that I may be let out?\"", "We had tried to lighten his confinement by seeing that he was well supplied with food, and his Father and Paquette had paid him visits from time to time. But in spite of these attentions, and in spite of the kindness he received at the Fort, his confinement was irksome beyond anything he could say."],
  p3s42: ["On the morning of a bright autumn day, word came to the authorities that the chiefs of the nation would present themselves at the Agency to deliver the suspected people as prisoners to the Americans.", "At ten o'clock, looking out along the Portage road, we could make out a moving concourse of people, brilliant with color and with glittering arms, and, as it came nearer, certain white objects among them of a kind we had never seen in such a procession before.", "General Dodge, Major Plympton, and one or two other officers took their seats with Mr. Kinzie on the platform in front of the door of our mansion to receive them, while we stationed ourselves at the window, where we could both see and hear.", "The procession wound up the hill and came on toward us, marching slowly. It was a grand and a solemn sight. First came some of the principal chiefs in their most brilliant array. Behind them the prisoners, every one of them dressed in white cotton, in token of their innocence, with girdles around their waists. The drum and the shee-shee-qua kept time under the death-song they were chaunting as they walked. They wore no paint and no ornaments. Their faces were grave and thoughtful. It might well be a serious moment to them, for they knew very little of the customs of the whites, and what little they did know was not of a kind to inspire cheerfulness. Nothing but their Father's assurance that they should receive strict justice would likely have brought them to fulfil the engagement of the nation in this manner.", "The rest of the procession was made up of a long train of Winnebagoes, all decked out in their holiday garb.", "The chiefs came up and shook hands with the gentlemen, who stood ready to receive their greeting. Then the prisoners came forward and went through the same salutation with the officers. When they offered their hands to their Father, he declined them.", "\"No,\" he said. \"You have come here accused of great crimes — of having assisted in taking the lives of some of the defenceless settlers. When you have been tried by the laws of the land, and been proved innocent, then your Father will give you his hand.\"", "They looked more serious still at this, as though it told them that their Father, too, believed them guilty. They stepped back a little and seated themselves on the ground in a row, without speaking, facing their Father and the officers. The other Indians all took seats in a circle around them — all except the one-eyed chief, Kau-ray-kau-say-kah, the White Crow, who had been deputed to deliver the prisoners to the Agent.", "He made a speech. His countrymen asserted their innocence of the charges brought against them, he said, but they were quite willing to be tried by the laws of white men. He hoped they would not be detained long, but that the matter would be investigated soon, and that they would come out of it clear and white.", "He was assured in reply that all things would be conducted fairly and impartially, exactly as if the accused were white men, and to that was added the hope that they would be found to have been good and true citizens, and peaceful children of their Great Father, the President.", "When this was over, White Crow asked permission to transfer the medal he had received from the President as a mark of friendship to his son, who stood beside him, and whom the nation had chosen to fill his place as chief, an office he wished to resign. The speeches made on this occasion, as Paquette interpreted them, the modest bearing of the young man, and the dignified yet feeling manner of the father all through it, made the whole ceremony deeply impressive, and when the older man lifted the medal from his own neck and hung it around his son's, saying a few fitting words to him, I do not think anyone there could have watched it unmoved.", "I had been watching the faces of the prisoners as they sat on the ground in front of me, through all of these ceremonies. With one exception they were open and calm and expressive of conscious innocence. About that one I could not help admitting there might be reasonable doubt. One of them was remarkably fine-looking. Another was a boy of certainly not more than seventeen, and all through the transfer of the medal he looked from one speaker to the other, listening to what was said with an air and an expression of almost childlike interest and satisfaction.", "Our hearts felt sad for them when, the ceremonies over, they were led away by a file of soldiers and committed to the dungeon of the guard-house, to stay there until such time as they should be summoned before the court appointed to try their cause."],
  p3s43: ["The ceremonies of the surrender were gone through, and still the Indians did not disperse. They stayed on near the Portage, camped in the constant expectation of the annuity money they had been summoned there to receive. The day on which Governor Porter was to set out and bring it was put off a week, and then another week, and then another. I have thought since about what he might have done differently if he could have seen, from where he sat, the whole length of what that delay was going to cost. He would possibly have been prompter. He was not.", "There were several reasons why an early payment mattered. The Winnebagoes had abandoned their own grounds that spring out of a desperate anxiety not to appear, to any white eye, to be fraternizing with the Sauks. So they had planted nothing that year. No gardens. No corn-fields. They had no provisions in hand either for the day they were living in or for the winter coming, nothing but their scanty stores of wild rice, and the rice was going down while they sat at the Portage and waited. If the hunting failed through the winter and the spring, they would have nothing at all.", "In the second place, the rations the Agent had been permitted by the Government to deal out to them now and then had been cut off. The Commissary had run short. The frequent levies of militia through the summer campaign, and the reinforcement of the garrison by the troops from Fort Howard, had drawn so heavily on the stores at this post that everything issued now had to be counted out with the most rigid economy.", "My husband had seen it coming. As soon as the war ended he commissioned Mr. Kercheval, then sutler at Fort Howard, to procure him a couple of boat-loads of corn, to be distributed among the Indians. Unfortunately there was no corn to be had out of Michigan. It had to be brought from Ohio, and in those days business was never done in a hurry; and by the time it finally reached Green Bay the navigation of the Fox River had closed over it. It sat there. It would be brought up the following spring.", "Every day wore on and the silver did not appear. At last their Father advised them to scatter to their hunting-grounds and get food, with the promise that they should be summoned the instant Governor Porter arrived. They took the advice and went.", "While they had been in our neighborhood they had asked, more than once, for leave to dance the scalp-dance in front of our door. I know nothing that can be imagined to match it. The scalps are stretched on little hoops, or frames, and carried on the ends of slender poles, and these are brandished about through the figures of the dance, with cries and shouts and furious gestures. The women begin as spectators. Then the scene takes them, and the music, which their own discordant notes are making more deafening by the minute, and they rush in, seize the scalps out of the hands of the owners, and toss them frantically about, screaming and yelling. I have seen as many as forty or fifty scalps in a single dance. On one occasion a man came quite near me carrying his, and I looked up, and saw that the hair on it was long and fair, and had plainly belonged to a woman. Another carried the skin of a human hand, stretched and prepared with as much care as if it had been some costly jewel. Sometimes they danced by moonlight. Those were the worst."],
  p3s44: ["Among so many painful events there were now and then some that came very near the ludicrous.", "One evening, sitting at tea, we heard guns firing off in the direction of the Wisconsin. Everyone was on their feet at once, making instinctively for the garrison. We came out of the house into a bluff and a meadow already in full commotion — Indians running toward the sound with their guns and spears laid across their shoulders, squaws and children standing out in front of their lodges and staring anxiously toward the noise that nobody could account for, and knots of French and half-breeds doing exactly what we were doing, running for the bridge to get themselves inside the pickets that had been put up so lately.", "One company of Indians came past us at speed, and a weapon carried carelessly caught one of our party on the side of the head. \"Oh!\" she shrieked, \"I am killed! an Indian has tomahawked me!\" She was reassured only by discovering that she could still run as fast as the best of us.", "When we were inside the Fort and standing on the parade-ground we could not help laughing at the figures we cut. Some of us had come without hats or shawls. Others had bundles of valuables snatched up in the moment of leaving. One was still holding her piece of bread-and-butter, not having had the presence of mind to put it down before she took to flight.", "The alarm turned out to have come from a party of Winnebagoes out of one of the Barribault villages. They were about to leave their home for a time, and were going through the ceremony of burying the scalps that they and their fathers had taken.", "Like the military funerals of civilized nations, their solemnities closed with the discharge of several volleys over the grave of their trophies."],
  p3s45: ["At last, about the beginning of November, two months after the time appointed, Governor Porter came in with the annuity money, and Major Forsyth and Mr. Kercheval with him. The Indians were assembled again, the payment was made, and they laid in a larger quantity of ammunition than usual — they understood well enough that only a good hunt would make up what was already lost — and then they set off for their wintering grounds.", "We were changing our own quarters about then, to our no small satisfaction. Through all the disturbances of the summer the new Agency House had been going quietly up — permission to build it had come from the Government at last, after a great deal of delay — and soon after the Governor and his party left, we moved into it.", "We had been settled a few weeks when Lieutenant Davies appeared one morning, just as we were sitting down to breakfast, with consternation all over his face. The Indian prisoners had escaped from the black-hole. The commanding officer, Colonel Cutler, wanted Mr. Kinzie to come over to the Fort and counsel with him about what was to be done.", "They had probably begun work very soon after they were put down there — the black-hole is a dungeon in the basement of the guard-house. What they noticed first was the regularity of it: meals brought three times a day, and in between, no one at all. So they went at the wall with their knives, and the earth they drew out they scattered thin across the floor of the prison. A blanket went over the opening, and one of the company always sat on the blanket, in place well before the hour when the soldier who had charge of them was due. Every time he came the Indians were sitting quietly, smoking, in perfect order. There was never anything about them to excite suspicion.", "They had never read the memoirs of Baron Trenck. What they had done was watch the proceedings of the badgers. Profiting by that example, they shaped the opening as they went, working it in a spiral, and in about six weeks the end of it came up into the open air beyond the walls of the Fort.", "They left their blankets behind so as to be as little encumbered as possible in flight. It was bitter December weather. They went out into the woods and prairies in nothing but their calico shirts and leggings, and I can readily believe that hope and exultation kept them comfortably warm until they reached shelter among their friends.", "It would compromise our own reputation as loyal and patriotic citizens to say how much this news privately delighted us.", "The question was how to get the fugitives back. The Agent could promise no more than to communicate with the chiefs and lay before them the wish of the officers — that the young men should give themselves up a second time, and so clear the men who had had charge of them of the imputation of carelessness the Government would otherwise be very likely to fasten upon them.", "On New-Year's Day many of the chiefs came in to the Agency, as their custom was, and their Father put the matter to them.", "They answered that if they saw the young men they would tell them what the officers would like them to do. Beyond that they could do nothing. They had kept their engagement; they had brought them in once and put them into the officers' hands. The Government had had them in its power and could not keep them. It must now go and catch them itself.", "The Government, having acquired a certain amount of experience the past summer in the business of catching Indians, wisely concluded to let the matter drop."],
  p3s46: ["About this time another thing happened that put our little community in no small excitement. Robineau, the striker from the blacksmith establishment at Sugar Creek, near the Four Lakes, came into the Agency on a very cold day. He had come for medical aid for Mâtâ's eldest daughter, Sophy, who had been sliding on the lake and had fallen on the ice and been badly hurt. Her father was away — he had gone to Prairie du Chien to put his youngest daughter in school. Two or three days had passed since the accident, a high fever had set in, and the girl was in great suffering, so it had been thought best to send Robineau to us for advice and aid and leave Turcotte and a friendly Indian woman from a neighboring lodge to take charge of her.", "The commanding officer, when the case was laid before him, did not think it prudent to let the surgeon leave the post, but he very cheerfully granted leave of absence to Currie, the hospital steward, a young man with some knowledge of medicine and surgery.", "Sophy would want an experienced nurse, so we secured Madame Bellaire, wife of the Frenchman generally employed as express to Chicago; and for help and company we added Agathe, a daughter of Day-kau-ray, who lived in Paquette's family.", "Of Agathe I shall have more to say hereafter.", "The cold was excessive the day Robineau, Currie, and the two women set out for Sugar Creek, about forty miles off. We had given them a good store of rice, crackers, tea, and sugar for the invalid, and all of it, with their own provisions for the road, was packed on the horse Robineau had ridden up to the Portage. They were expected to reach the place on the second day.", "On the fourth day after they left, Turcotte walked in. He had come to ask why Robineau had not returned with help for poor Sophy. There was only one way to read it. Robineau had guided them as ill as he had guided the boat at the Grande Chûte the summer before, and though he could not shipwreck them out on the prairie, he had certainly lost them somewhere in the woods. The comfort was that they could not well starve. The rice and crackers were several days' provisions, and with Agathe along, who must be used to this kind of life, they could hardly fail to come upon Indians in time and be brought back to the Portage.", "Still, day followed day and no word came. Turcotte went back to Sugar Creek with comforts and prescriptions for Sophy, and Colonel Cutler sent out a party to look for the missing ones. Of them all it was Currie we pitied most, with his delicate constitution.", "The snow fell, and the wind got up and howled, and we could settle to nothing but walking from window to window, watching to see whether anybody would come into sight out of the distance. There were no Indians near enough to send out after them. By the tenth day we had almost given them up.", "That was when the joyful news was suddenly brought in to us. They are found. They are at the Fort. A party of soldiers who had been out exploring out searching had come on them at Hastings's Woods, twelve miles off, working their way back toward the Portage slowly and feebly. They knew they had the right track under them by then. They had barely the strength to keep to it.", "They were worn out with cold and hunger. Their provisions had given out two days before, and they had talked seriously of killing the horse and eating him. The only thing that had stopped them was that Currie could not go on foot, and they dreaded having to leave him in the woods to perish.", "Agathe had been sure from the first that they were on the wrong track, but Robineau, with his usual obstinacy, kept to it until it brought them out at the Rock River. Then he was obliged to admit his error, and they turned and began retracing their steps.", "Agathe had carried her hatchet with her, as her people always do. So they had a fire every night, and boughs cut to shelter them from the storms. Without that they must certainly have perished.", "Two things in the story stirred something in us stronger than sympathy. The first was that the miserable Robineau had demanded of Currie, first all his money, and then his watch, as the price of bringing the party back into the right path — which he claimed all along that he knew perfectly well.", "The second was Bellaire, who gave his kind, excellent wife a hearty flogging for going off, as he put it, on such a fool's errand.", "The latter culprit was out of our jurisdiction and stayed out of it. Monsieur Robineau was discharged on the spot, and warned that he might think himself a happy man to have escaped a legal process for swindling.", "I am happy to say that Sophy Mâtâ, in whose behalf all this suffering had been undergone, was quite recovered by the time her father came back from the Prairie."],
  p3s47: ["Agathe was the daughter of an Indian who went by the name of Rascal Day-kau-ray. Whether he had earned the name is a thing the reader may judge for himself before the end of this. He was brother to the grand old chief of that name, and as unlike him as two men of one blood can be.", "The Day-kau-rays were a very handsome family, and this daughter was remarkable even among them. She was tall, and well made, with a round, sweet face and the peculiarly soft, melodious voice that belongs to the women of her people — enough by itself to make a stranger look twice. But it was the pensive set of her face that drew you, and made you want to know what had put it there. I had the history from her friend Mrs. Paquette, and it was a touching one.", "A young officer at the Fort had seen her, and had set — I will not say his heart, it may be doubted whether he had one — but his mind upon her. He went to Paquette and asked him to negotiate what he was pleased to call a marriage with her. I am sorry to write that Paquette allowed himself to be brought into the scheme. He knew exactly what he was doing when he carried false representations to Agathe's family. He knew exactly what he was bringing down on her.", "The girl was already betrothed, to a young man of her own people, and, as is generally the way, the attachment on both sides was very strong. Among these simple people, who have few subjects of thought or speculation outside the interests of their daily life, their affections and their animosities make up the warp and woof of what they are. All their feelings run intense because they are concentrated on so few objects. With the women particularly, the ties of family take up the whole amount of their sensibilities.", "The marriage connection among them is sacred and cannot be dissolved. I have read, in a recent report to the Historical Society of Wisconsin, that in former times a temporary marriage between a white man and a Menomonee woman was no uncommon thing, and that such an arrangement brought no scandal with it. I am afraid that if such cases were looked into, a good deal of deceit and misrepresentation would be found added to the other sins of the transaction, and that the woman would turn out to have been a victim in it and not a willing party.", "In any case, no system of the kind exists among the Winnebagoes. The strictest sense of female propriety is a distinguishing trait among them. A woman who transgresses it is said to have \"forgotten herself,\" and she is certain to be cast off and forgotten by her friends.", "The marriage proposed between the young officer and the daughter of Day-kau-ray was understood on their side to be a true one and a lasting one. The father would never have laid himself open to the contempt of his whole nation by selling his daughter to be any man's mistress. The Day-kau-rays, as I have said elsewhere, were not a little proud of the remote cross of French blood that ran mixed with the aboriginal stream in their veins, and probably, in acceding to the connection, Agathe's father was moved quite as much by the honor he believed it carried as by the amount of valuable presents that came with the overtures made to him.", "Be that as it may. The girl was torn from the man she loved and handed over from her father's lodge to the young officer's quarters.", "There were no ladies in the garrison at that time. Had there been, the step would hardly have been ventured. Far off in the wilderness, shut away from the wholesome influences of religion and society, what wonder that the moral sense sometimes goes blind, and that a man makes the choice, \"Evil, be thou my good!\"", "The first wrong was followed by a second, worse in its cruelty. The young officer left the post on furlough, as he said. He never came back. In time the news arrived that he was married, and when he rejoined his regiment it was at another post.", "There was a natural feeling behind the strength of the woe pronounced against him, and by more tongues than one. \"He will never,\" said my informant, \"dare show himself in this country again! Not an Indian who knows the Day-kau-rays but would take his life if he should meet him!\""],
  p3s48: ["Every tie poor Agathe had was broken but the one that held her to her infant. She never went back to her father's lodge. She had been deserted, and she felt that being deserted she was dishonored. All the ambition she had left was to bring the child up as the whites brought up theirs. She dressed it in the costume of the French children, a frock of bright calico and a cap of the same stuff trimmed with narrow black lace. It was a fine child. The only time I ever saw a smile cross that woman's face was when somebody in our family praised it or took it up.", "Even that, the one source of happiness she had, Agathe was required to give up. While we were away at Green Bay, with the Sauks in the neighborhood, the child was taken violently ill. Paquette's house, which was the mother's home, was full of Indians coming and going, and the noise and disturbance in it were constant. My husband had a place made ready for her under our own roof where it would be quiet and where the post physician could attend her. It was all no use. Nothing could save the little creature's life. They told me afterward about the mother's agony as she hung over the only thing she had on the earth, and I am told there was no bearing to look at it. When they took the child from her at last, it seemed almost more than nature could carry. There were friends about her, not of her nation or her color, who tried to comfort her. Did the father ever send so much as a thought or an inquiry after what became of his child, or after the young creature whose life he had made dark and desolate? We will hope that he did. That he repented, and asked pardon from above for the evil he had worked.", "Agathe had been baptized by M. Mazzuchelli. It may be that she had gathered some religious knowledge out of it that could console her in her sorrow, and make up to her for hopes and joys blasted so early.", "Some months after the child's death she came, with several of the half-breed women of the neighborhood, to pay me a visit of respect and congratulation on the arrival of the young Shaw-nee-aw-kee. She looked at her \"little brother,\" as they were calling him. She took his soft, tiny hand inside her own. The tears stood in her eyes, and she said a few small words of tenderness that showed how full her heart was. I could hardly keep from mingling my tears with hers, thinking on the whole sum of sorrow and desolation that one man's selfishness had brought about."],
  p3s49: ["Early in February, 1833, my husband and Lieutenant Hunter, in company with one or two others, set off on a trip to Chicago. The place had grown so much of a town — it may have held fifty inhabitants — that the proprietors of \"Kinzie's Addition\" found it necessary to lay out lots and open streets through their property. It was all accomplished during that visit.", "While they were on the ground with a surveyor, my husband's attention was caught by a very bright-looking boy in Indian dress, who went hopping along beside the assistant carrying the chain and mimicking him as he called out, \"Stick!\" \"stuck!\" He asked who the lad was, and learned, to his surprise, that he was the brother of our old family servants Victoire, Genevieve, and Baptiste. Tomah — that was his name — had never worn civilized clothing in his life. He was in blanket and leggings, and had always lived in a wigwam. My husband asked him whether he would like to come to Fort Winnebago and learn to be a white boy. The idea pleased him greatly, and his mother giving her consent to the arrangement, he was packed into a wagon with the two gentlemen and their traveling gear when they set out on the return trip.", "Tomah had been fitted out with a jacket and trousers and the other articles of clothing his new sphere and character required. They were coming up on the Aux Plaines, approaching the residence of Glode (Claude) Laframboise, where Tomah knew he would meet acquaintances of his own. He asked leave to get down out of the wagon and walk a little way. When the gentlemen next laid eyes on him he was in full Pottowattamie costume. It was bitter winter weather, and he had put his uncomfortable native garb back on rather than let his old friends see him in a state of transformation."],
  p3s50: ["The first thing we did when he reached Fort Winnebago was to furnish him with a complete wardrobe, which we put in a box in his sleeping-apartment and placed under his own charge. There are no words for what that did to him. The valuable possessions were his, and every spare minute he had went to the contemplation of them. Now and then Tomah would go missing. He was always found in the same place, seated beside his little trunk, folding and refolding the clothes, laying them in lengthwise, then taking them out and laying them crosswise, the happiest of mortals.", "Our next step was to teach him to be useful. The little offices given him at first were chosen as things not altogether strange to him, but we soon noticed a pattern: whenever there was anything in the shape of work in prospect, Tomah slipped off to bed, sometimes before he had even had his supper. One evening some fish were given him to scale. It was just at dark, and Tom, according to custom, retired at once to bed.", "The cook came in to ask what was to be done about it. I had to call in my husband as interpreter. He sent for Tomah, and when the boy came into the parlor Mr. Kinzie said to him, in Pottowattamie, \"There are some fish, Tomah, in the kitchen, and we want you to scale them.\"", "\"Now?\" said Tom, with an expression of pure amazement. \"It is very late.\"", "A young lady who was visiting us, Miss Rolette, understood the language, and she could not help bursting out laughing at the simplicity of it, and we joined her out of sympathy. Tom looked a little indignant at that. But when it was explained to him that it was the white custom to scale fish at night, and to put salt and pepper on them, he was soon reconciled to doing his duty in the matter.", "His next office was to lay the table. There was a best service of china that only came out when we had company, and a best set of teaspoons that I kept in the drawer of a bureau in my own room, above-stairs. I was in the habit of keeping that drawer locked and putting the key under a small clock on the mantel-piece. The first time I showed Tomah how to arrange things for visitors, I brought the silver down and put it on the table myself.", "Soon after, we were to have company to tea again, and I explained to Tomah that the best china must be used. Imagine my surprise, going through the dining-room a little while later, at finding not only the new china laid out but the company silver beside it. I asked our mother, who could speak with him, to inquire into the matter.", "Tomah said, very coolly, \"He got the silver where it was kept.\"", "\"Did he find the drawer open?\"", "\"No — he opened it with a key.\"", "\"Was the key in the drawer?\"", "\"No — it was under that thing on the shelf.\"", "\"How did he know it was kept there?\"", "That was the one thing Mr. Tomah declined to tell. We never did find out whether he had watched my movements at some time or other. Nobody had ever seen him in that part of the house. And yet there was scarcely an article in it whose whereabouts Tomah did not know. When anybody was at a loss to find something, it was always the same answer — ask Tomah, he will tell you. And in fact he always did.", "He was a subject of much amusement to the young officers. We were to have a tea-party one evening, all the families and the young officers from the Fort. To make Tomah's appearance as professional as possible we made him a white apron with long sleeves to wear while he helped Mary and Josette carry the tea round, for I have to admit that Tomah's clothes were not kept in such nice order out of the trunk as in it.", "Tom was delighted with the new costume and with the new employment. He acquitted himself to perfection; he never had any difficulty imitating what he had seen another person do. After tea we had some music. I was standing by the piano, where one of the ladies was seated, when Lieutenant Vancleve said to me in a low voice, \"Look behind you a moment.\"", "I turned. There sat Tom, between two of the company, as stately as could be, his white apron smoothed down over his knees and his hands clasped in front of him, listening to the music, on the best possible terms with himself and with everyone in the room. Julian and Edwin were hardly able to hold their merriment in, but they were afraid to do or say anything that might make him move before the company had had the full enjoyment of the scene. It was voted unanimously that Tomah should be allowed to stay and enjoy the pleasures of society for one evening. But he had his own restlessness in him, and the moment the music stopped he was tired of it, and took his leave of the company without ceremony."],
  p3s51: ["Spring came on, and with it the thing we had spent the whole winter dreading. We learned how bad it had become from the ones who came to the door — small parties, a few at a time, asking for food.", "Their Father gave out rations for as long as there were rations to give. But the stores in the Commissary Department had run down so far that Colonel Cutler could not in conscience authorize more than the barest relief, and that only where the case was already desperate.", "We had been careful with our own stores since the first cold, afraid of reaching the bottom of the flour and the meal before more could be brought up from \"below.\" We had even bought a quantity of sour flour that the commissary had condemned; with enough saleratus worked into it and a fair proportion of potatoes, it made a bread we were glad enough to eat. But party after party came and stood before us and told us plainly what they were enduring, and we gave, and gave again, until the day came when there was nothing left in the house to hand over.", "The half-breed families near us had done as we had, sharing while their own stock lasted, and now they too were obliged to refuse. Their women came often to sit with us and repeat what was being carried in from the wintering grounds. The winter had been an open one. The snow had never lain deep enough at any time for the Indians to track deer, and in any case there were no deer; the troops and the war-parties had swept the country clean the summer before.", "They were dying in whole companies, we heard, of nothing but want of food, and lying stretched where they fell along the road to the Portage, which they had been trying to reach, dragging frames that had no strength left in them. For weeks together some had lived on soup boiled from the bark of the slippery elm, or on stewed acorns.", "Our own food came now as a daily ration from the garrison; matters had reached the point where a barrel of flour could not be had at one time. When a meal was finished I went into the pantry and gathered every last particle that remained, and set it aside, because someone would be at the door needing it, and someone always was."],
  p3s52: ["One day, while I was doing this, a face appeared at the window that I had once known well. It was the pretty daughter of the elder Day-kau-ray. She used to visit us often, and would watch everything we did with great interest — our sewing, our weeding and cultivating in the garden, our reading. Of the reading I had tried many times to give her some idea, showing her the plates in the Family Bible and doing my best to explain them. But of late I had quite lost sight of her. Now, how changed she was, how wan. I said what I always said to her — \"Tshah-ko-zhah?\" — what is it — and she gave a sigh that was nearly a sob. She did not beg. Her face said the whole of it.", "I took up my dish and handed it out to her, and I expected to see her fall on the contents where she stood. She did not. She took it and made signs that she would soon come back, and walked away with it. When she brought it back to me I was almost certain she had not tasted a morsel of it herself.", "Oh, the boats. The boats with the corn in them. Why did they not come? We wrote, and we sent, and we begged them to hurry, and everything and everybody in those unenterprising times moved so slowly that it made no difference at all. We could be certain only of this: that the boats would come when they were ready and not one moment before."],
  p3s53: ["Before long we were obliged to keep the doors and the windows fast, to shut out a sight of misery we had no means of relieving. If a door was opened to let in a member of the family, some wretched mother would push through it, take hold of my infant's hand and put the hand of her own famishing child inside it, and tell us, pleading, that he was imploring \"his little brother\" for food. The stoutest man alive could not have watched that with a dry eye, and it happened again and again. Screening the lower part of the windows with curtains did no good. They climbed up the outside, and tier upon tier of gaunt, wretched faces would rise above the curtain and look in at us, watching, to see whether we were truly as ill provided as we said we were.", "The noble old Day-kau-ray came in one day from the Barribault to tell us what state his village was in. More than forty of his people, he said, had now gone many days with nothing to eat but bark and roots. My husband took him to the commanding officer so he could tell his story himself and learn whether any quantity of food could be had from that quarter. The result was a promise of a small allowance of flour, enough to take the edge off the hunger of his own family.", "When this was explained to the chief, he turned away from it. No, he said. If his people could not be relieved, then he and his family would starve along with them. And he refused the offered succor, for those nearest and dearest to him, until every one of them could share alike."],
  p3s54: ["When the announcement came at last — that the boats were in sight — it was a thrilling and a most joyful sound.", "Hundreds of poor creatures gathered on the bank to watch them arrive. It was torture to see how slowly they came, following the winding course of the river through the whole width of that prairie. We were watching with an anxiety and impatience matched only by that of the sufferers themselves; and yet, when the first boat touched the land, we could scarcely keep from laughing, because old Wild-Cat — who had fallen off considerably from his huge amount of flesh — seized \"the Washington Woman\" in his arms and hugged her and danced her about in the ecstasy of his delight.", "Their Father made a sign for them all to fall to with their hatchets, which they had been holding ready a long while, and in an incredibly short time barrel after barrel of corn was broken open and emptied out. Even the little children got themselves pans and kettles full of it and hurried away to the fires blazing all around, to parch and cook what they had seized.", "From that time there was no more destitution. The present abundance was followed immediately by the arrival of supplies for the Commissary's Department, and, refreshed and strengthened again, our poor children went back to their villages to get their crops ready for the coming season."],
  p3s55: ["In the course of the spring we had a visit from the Rev. Mr. Kent and Mrs. Kent, of Galena. The occasion is memorable, being the first on which the gospel, according to the Protestant faith, was preached at Fort Winnebago. The large parlor of the hospital was fitted up for the service, and we said it to each other gladly — let us go to the house of the Lord.", "For nearly three years we had lived here with no public service of praise and thanksgiving at all. We took this beginning as an omen of better times, and our little sewing-society worked with renewed industry to raise a fund that might one day secure the permanent services of a missionary.", "Not long after, on a fine spring morning, while we were seated at breakfast, a party of Indians came into the parlor and up to the door of the room where we were. Two of them went on through and out onto the small portico. The third stayed where he was, standing in the doorway where he had first appeared. He was almost directly opposite me. I raised my eyes, and in spite of the change of dress and the paint he was covered in, I knew him at once.", "I went on pouring the coffee. As I poured I said to my husband, \"The one behind you, with whom you are speaking, is one of the escaped prisoners.\"", "Mr. Kinzie did not turn his head. He went on listening to all the directions they were giving him about the repairing of their guns and traps and the rest of what they wanted left with the blacksmith. When they had run on a while he turned carelessly toward the parlor door and answered the one who was speaking to him. Then he spoke to me again, and what he said was, \"You are right, but it is no affair of ours. We are none of us to look so as to give him notice that we suspect anything. They are undoubtedly innocent, and have suffered enough already.\"", "Contrary to his usual custom, their Father did not ask their names. He wrote out their directions, tied each one to the implement it belonged with, and told them to go and deliver them to M. Morrin themselves.", "The rest of our circle were greatly pleased by the young fellow's audacity, and we quite longed to tell the officers that we could have caught one of their fugitives for them, if we had had a mind to."],
  p3s56: ["It had come to that point at last: we began to talk in earnest about giving up the home we loved and settling ourselves at Detroit for a time, while the arrangements went forward for a permanent place at Chicago.", "When the news reached our Winnebago children, the grief of it was loud and it did not stop. They came in from all the country round about, wanting to know whether what they had heard was true, and begging us — pleading, in earnest — that we would stay and live and die among them.", "Of all of them, none was so broken by it as Elizabeth, our poor Cut-Nose. On the day we first told her, she sat down in one spot and stayed there hour after hour, taking the corner of the chintz shawl she wore pinned across her bosom to blot the tears as fast as they came down her face.", "\"No! I never, never, never shall I find such friends again,\" she would exclaim. \"You will go away, and I shall be left here all alone.\"", "Wild-Cat grieved too — fat, jolly Wild-Cat — and he made no attempt to grieve quietly.", "On the morning we left, he would not be satisfied until he had the baby in his arms and settled on his fat, dirty knee. \"Oh, my little brother,\" he told him, \"you will never come back to see your poor brother again!\"", "He had taken an extra glass for the occasion, and he cried like an infant.", "So on the morning of the 1st of July, 1888, with heavy hearts, we said our good-byes to the long cortége that had walked us down to the boat. She was waiting to carry us to Green Bay, where Governor Porter and Mr. Brush would meet us and take us on to Detroit under their escort.", "Their farewells finished, they turned about to go with their father across the Portage, on his road to Chicago. We watched them a long while, the line of them bending along the road, and we heard them wailing still — a parting they knew already was for good."],
};
